WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.430 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:02.430 --> 00:00:03.888 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.888 --> 00:00:06.318 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:06.318 --> 00:00:16.477 align:middle line:90%


00:00:16.477 --> 00:00:18.060 align:middle line:84%
GLENN ELLISON: So
bounded rationality.

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:21.937 align:middle line:84%
So we start that, you
know, there's really,

00:00:21.937 --> 00:00:24.020 align:middle line:84%
if you go back and look
through the IO literature,

00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:26.420 align:middle line:84%
there's this very, very
long history of studying

00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:29.960 align:middle line:90%
bounded rationality.

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:32.840 align:middle line:84%
In some sense, it
starts at the same time

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:35.780 align:middle line:84%
that Samuelson was having
of revolution in going

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:39.080 align:middle line:84%
towards rational economics,
the entire time there

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:42.200 align:middle line:84%
was active debate about
whether we should be modeling

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:44.130 align:middle line:90%
things rationally or not.

00:00:44.130 --> 00:00:47.000 align:middle line:84%
And if you look at the early
IO with bounded rationality,

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:48.800 align:middle line:84%
actually the
difference from today

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:52.310 align:middle line:84%
is there's an awful lot of
IO with bounded rationality

00:00:52.310 --> 00:00:53.440 align:middle line:90%
on the firm side.

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:56.780 align:middle line:84%
There's a lot of skepticism
of this idea of firms

00:00:56.780 --> 00:00:59.660 align:middle line:84%
as sophisticated,
profit-maximizing entities

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:01.260 align:middle line:90%
that just always do what's best.

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:06.470 align:middle line:84%
And so in the 1940s, there was
this debate of should we think

00:01:06.470 --> 00:01:09.050 align:middle line:84%
of firms as maximizing profits
or should we think of firms

00:01:09.050 --> 00:01:13.430 align:middle line:84%
as trying to get a pretty good
profit that's secure and not

00:01:13.430 --> 00:01:14.730 align:middle line:90%
trying to maximize profits?

00:01:14.730 --> 00:01:17.490 align:middle line:90%


00:01:17.490 --> 00:01:20.760 align:middle line:84%
There was also a lot of
literature about this team

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:27.120 align:middle line:84%
theory or Simon about firms have
to process information that's

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:31.050 align:middle line:84%
really complicated, just like
firms have to manufacture goods,

00:01:31.050 --> 00:01:33.100 align:middle line:84%
firms have to deal
with information.

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:36.010 align:middle line:84%
They have bounded information
processing capabilities.

00:01:36.010 --> 00:01:38.170 align:middle line:84%
They organize
themselves to do this,

00:01:38.170 --> 00:01:40.920 align:middle line:84%
and there are costs of
trying to set the right price

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:42.922 align:middle line:90%
or costs of--

00:01:42.922 --> 00:01:45.130 align:middle line:84%
just like there are costs
of trying to produce goods,

00:01:45.130 --> 00:01:48.240 align:middle line:84%
and we should take that
those costs into account.

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:50.370 align:middle line:84%
I'm actually going to
talk a little bit more

00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:52.330 align:middle line:84%
about Smallwood and
Conlisk on Wednesday,

00:01:52.330 --> 00:01:55.140 align:middle line:84%
but Smallwood and Conlisk
were very clear arguing,

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:57.450 align:middle line:84%
when first rational
consumers were

00:01:57.450 --> 00:02:00.930 align:middle line:84%
coming in, that they felt
rational consumers were much

00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:02.920 align:middle line:84%
less plausible than
irrational consumers

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:05.910 align:middle line:84%
and we shouldn't go all the
way to assuming that consumers

00:02:05.910 --> 00:02:11.009 align:middle line:84%
are these hyper sophisticated,
rational people.

00:02:11.009 --> 00:02:14.220 align:middle line:84%
More recent literature has
really, over the last decades,

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:17.930 align:middle line:84%
has focused on abandon
the irrational firms

00:02:17.930 --> 00:02:20.390 align:middle line:84%
and gone with hyper
sophisticated firms,

00:02:20.390 --> 00:02:24.380 align:middle line:84%
but firms exploiting
boundedly rational customers.

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:26.960 align:middle line:84%
That's a big literature
and there's a thought of,

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:29.360 align:middle line:84%
you know, this is why we
want to have a consumer

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:31.640 align:middle line:84%
financial protection
bureau or other agencies,

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:35.270 align:middle line:84%
because firms are going to try
to exploit irrational consumers

00:02:35.270 --> 00:02:38.210 align:middle line:90%
and that creates inefficiencies.

00:02:38.210 --> 00:02:40.190 align:middle line:84%
These inefficiencies
can be large because--

00:02:40.190 --> 00:02:44.330 align:middle line:84%
like normal inefficiencies are
triangles of price are too high,

00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:46.760 align:middle line:84%
consumers who have a
value a bit above cost

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:48.280 align:middle line:90%
don't get to purchase things.

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:50.030 align:middle line:84%
If you put in boundedly
rational consumers

00:02:50.030 --> 00:02:52.040 align:middle line:84%
and consumers are buying
things they don't want

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:54.320 align:middle line:84%
or paying much more than
something is worth to them,

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:56.540 align:middle line:84%
you can get bigger
inefficiencies.

00:02:56.540 --> 00:02:59.150 align:middle line:84%
And I would say there's three
different approaches here

00:02:59.150 --> 00:03:01.850 align:middle line:90%
that you see taken.

00:03:01.850 --> 00:03:04.820 align:middle line:84%
One is the rule
of thumb approach,

00:03:04.820 --> 00:03:07.580 align:middle line:84%
following Smallwood and
Conlisk, which just says,

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:09.410 align:middle line:84%
rather than modeling
consumers as rational,

00:03:09.410 --> 00:03:13.010 align:middle line:84%
let's just say what we think
is a reasonable model for what

00:03:13.010 --> 00:03:14.930 align:middle line:84%
consumers would actually
do in a situation

00:03:14.930 --> 00:03:17.480 align:middle line:84%
like a consumer walks
into a restaurant.

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:21.200 align:middle line:84%
Do they really have a utility
for every different thing

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:23.583 align:middle line:84%
on the menu and have beliefs
about how much they're

00:03:23.583 --> 00:03:25.250 align:middle line:84%
going to like everything
they don't have

00:03:25.250 --> 00:03:26.250 align:middle line:90%
and rationally maximize?

00:03:26.250 --> 00:03:28.563 align:middle line:84%
Or do people just
look at it quickly,

00:03:28.563 --> 00:03:30.230 align:middle line:84%
pick out something
they think they like,

00:03:30.230 --> 00:03:32.810 align:middle line:90%
and buy it, or whatever.

00:03:32.810 --> 00:03:34.890 align:middle line:84%
The explicit
bounded rationality.

00:03:34.890 --> 00:03:37.430 align:middle line:84%
In some sense, search
theory is a model

00:03:37.430 --> 00:03:41.000 align:middle line:84%
of explicit bounded rationality
that consumers may be rational

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:44.400 align:middle line:84%
but have computational or
other informational costs,

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:46.850 align:middle line:84%
and they do this
combination of doing

00:03:46.850 --> 00:03:50.750 align:middle line:84%
the best thing they can given
their computational costs.

00:03:50.750 --> 00:03:54.420 align:middle line:84%
Search is one example,
but there are many others.

00:03:54.420 --> 00:03:57.740 align:middle line:84%
And then the third branch is the
psychology and economics branch

00:03:57.740 --> 00:04:00.290 align:middle line:84%
where this is building
off the kinds of things

00:04:00.290 --> 00:04:04.230 align:middle line:84%
you would see in a behavioral
economics class and modeling

00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:06.810 align:middle line:84%
consumers as having
behavioral biases

00:04:06.810 --> 00:04:09.600 align:middle line:84%
and thinking about how
firms exploit those.

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:13.150 align:middle line:84%
You know, this has been the most
active branch in recent years,

00:04:13.150 --> 00:04:15.090 align:middle line:84%
and so today's lecture,
I'm pretty much--

00:04:15.090 --> 00:04:17.673 align:middle line:84%
unless I get to the paper at the
end I think I won't have time

00:04:17.673 --> 00:04:20.700 align:middle line:84%
for, today is pretty much going
to be about these psychology

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:23.890 align:middle line:90%
and economics papers.

00:04:23.890 --> 00:04:24.490 align:middle line:90%
What goes on.

00:04:24.490 --> 00:04:28.210 align:middle line:84%
If you take a behavioral
economics class?

00:04:28.210 --> 00:04:30.270 align:middle line:84%
You know, so over
the last 25 years,

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:33.280 align:middle line:84%
there's been this explosion of
work in behavioral economics.

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:35.940 align:middle line:84%
And the way behavioral
economics classes normally go

00:04:35.940 --> 00:04:39.330 align:middle line:84%
is you talk about and read
psychological studies that

00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:43.170 align:middle line:84%
talk about how consumers do
things that are not rational,

00:04:43.170 --> 00:04:46.680 align:middle line:84%
and then you discuss
models that people

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:50.400 align:middle line:84%
have written to
model behavior that's

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:52.470 align:middle line:84%
kind of close to
rational but not quite

00:04:52.470 --> 00:04:55.990 align:middle line:84%
rational in a way that would
explain the psychological bias.

00:04:55.990 --> 00:04:59.040 align:middle line:84%
I mean, I guess Drew does a
little bit of this in 123.

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:03.180 align:middle line:90%
But it's you take a model that--

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:05.850 align:middle line:84%
take a standard model and then
you make some small tweak to it

00:05:05.850 --> 00:05:08.800 align:middle line:84%
that would allow consumers to
behave in some different way,

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:12.630 align:middle line:84%
and then get a utility-like
model that may have one

00:05:12.630 --> 00:05:14.890 align:middle line:84%
or two extra free
parameters that

00:05:14.890 --> 00:05:18.210 align:middle line:84%
could display this is nearly
rational but with these two

00:05:18.210 --> 00:05:19.960 align:middle line:84%
extra parameters
describing their behavior

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:23.892 align:middle line:84%
instead of behaving
fully rationally.

00:05:23.892 --> 00:05:27.880 align:middle line:84%
And there are many, many ways
in the behavioral economics

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:29.440 align:middle line:84%
literature in
which consumers are

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:32.500 align:middle line:84%
shown to behave not
rationally, and there

00:05:32.500 --> 00:05:36.020 align:middle line:84%
are rational-like
models for most of them.

00:05:36.020 --> 00:05:38.130 align:middle line:90%
So why don't I--

00:05:38.130 --> 00:05:41.590 align:middle line:84%
and, you know, the idea of
how this behavioral economics

00:05:41.590 --> 00:05:43.990 align:middle line:84%
literature could have an
influence is you write down

00:05:43.990 --> 00:05:46.318 align:middle line:84%
a nearly rational
model, and then

00:05:46.318 --> 00:05:48.610 align:middle line:84%
any model that you could
solve with rational consumers,

00:05:48.610 --> 00:05:50.693 align:middle line:84%
you can solve with these
nearly rational consumers

00:05:50.693 --> 00:05:52.732 align:middle line:84%
because you have a
well-specified thing

00:05:52.732 --> 00:05:54.190 align:middle line:84%
for their behavior
which isn't just

00:05:54.190 --> 00:05:56.290 align:middle line:90%
maximizing utility function.

00:05:56.290 --> 00:06:00.400 align:middle line:90%
So, for instance, loss aversion.

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:03.730 align:middle line:84%
In financial problems,
what do consumers do?

00:06:03.730 --> 00:06:07.486 align:middle line:90%
They're taking some action a.

00:06:07.486 --> 00:06:10.340 align:middle line:84%
And in the rational model,
if they take an action a,

00:06:10.340 --> 00:06:13.640 align:middle line:84%
they'll have some wealth w of
a which is a random variable,

00:06:13.640 --> 00:06:15.212 align:middle line:84%
and then they have
a utility that's

00:06:15.212 --> 00:06:16.670 align:middle line:84%
a function of that
wealth, and then

00:06:16.670 --> 00:06:19.460 align:middle line:84%
they maximize the expected
utility of the wealth

00:06:19.460 --> 00:06:22.160 align:middle line:90%
after taking the action.

00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:25.280 align:middle line:84%
In loss averse problems,
what is assumed

00:06:25.280 --> 00:06:29.000 align:middle line:84%
is that consumers have
a reference point, w0,

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:31.040 align:middle line:84%
which could be their
current wealth,

00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:35.090 align:middle line:84%
and they try to maximize
some expected function

00:06:35.090 --> 00:06:37.540 align:middle line:90%
of w of a minus w of 0.

00:06:37.540 --> 00:06:40.190 align:middle line:84%
So you have utility
not for wealth

00:06:40.190 --> 00:06:45.860 align:middle line:84%
but for changes in wealth,
and zero is a, somehow,

00:06:45.860 --> 00:06:48.600 align:middle line:90%
point that's special.

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:50.918 align:middle line:84%
And the simplest
version of this is

00:06:50.918 --> 00:06:52.460 align:middle line:84%
that you have a
utility function that

00:06:52.460 --> 00:07:01.490 align:middle line:84%
looks like this where you care
much more about losing money

00:07:01.490 --> 00:07:05.090 align:middle line:84%
than you care about making
money, and somehow losing money

00:07:05.090 --> 00:07:06.050 align:middle line:90%
is painful to you.

00:07:06.050 --> 00:07:07.280 align:middle line:90%
Because if you ask--

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:10.820 align:middle line:84%
there are all these experiments,
if you ask students which--

00:07:10.820 --> 00:07:16.580 align:middle line:84%
would you a 50/50 coin
flip, win $101, lose $100?

00:07:16.580 --> 00:07:20.390 align:middle line:84%
And almost everyone says, no, I
don't want to do the 50/50 coin

00:07:20.390 --> 00:07:23.820 align:middle line:84%
flip, win 101, lose 100,
or something like that.

00:07:23.820 --> 00:07:27.728 align:middle line:84%
It's not enough of a gain to
make it worth losing $100.

00:07:27.728 --> 00:07:30.020 align:middle line:84%
Matt Rabin has this very
beautiful paper on calibrating

00:07:30.020 --> 00:07:34.070 align:middle line:84%
preferences that says it's
just completely inconceivable

00:07:34.070 --> 00:07:38.540 align:middle line:84%
for a rational consumer to say
no to a 50/50, 101, 100 gamble,

00:07:38.540 --> 00:07:41.780 align:middle line:84%
because if you're going to turn
down a 50/50, 101, 100 gamble,

00:07:41.780 --> 00:07:47.450 align:middle line:84%
you would also turn down a lose
5,000, win a trillion gamble.

00:07:47.450 --> 00:07:49.520 align:middle line:84%
Because in some sense,
to turn down that gamble,

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:53.970 align:middle line:84%
you've got to have a very
concave utility function

00:07:53.970 --> 00:07:55.930 align:middle line:84%
and it's got to just
keep getting concave.

00:07:55.930 --> 00:07:59.340 align:middle line:84%
And if you don't like
small gambles, you can't--

00:07:59.340 --> 00:08:02.070 align:middle line:84%
obviously you all take million
dollar gambles with your lives,

00:08:02.070 --> 00:08:04.470 align:middle line:84%
like when you choose
your careers or whatever.

00:08:04.470 --> 00:08:06.840 align:middle line:84%
Somehow people treat
big stakes gambles

00:08:06.840 --> 00:08:09.390 align:middle line:84%
very different from
small stakes gambles.

00:08:09.390 --> 00:08:11.940 align:middle line:84%
This loss aversion
is one way to explain

00:08:11.940 --> 00:08:14.128 align:middle line:90%
why people might do that.

00:08:14.128 --> 00:08:16.170 align:middle line:84%
People often-- I guess,
more sophisticated models

00:08:16.170 --> 00:08:20.850 align:middle line:84%
do something more like
this as a utility function.

00:08:20.850 --> 00:08:23.697 align:middle line:84%
But the whole point is that
it does have a kink here where

00:08:23.697 --> 00:08:26.030 align:middle line:84%
the slope to the left and the
slope to the right differ.

00:08:26.030 --> 00:08:28.770 align:middle line:90%


00:08:28.770 --> 00:08:30.270 align:middle line:84%
When you have
multiple goods, you

00:08:30.270 --> 00:08:32.900 align:middle line:84%
have to assume, like, how
do we deal with like, OK,

00:08:32.900 --> 00:08:34.150 align:middle line:90%
this is the utility of wealth.

00:08:34.150 --> 00:08:35.733 align:middle line:84%
How would we deal
with multiple goods?

00:08:35.733 --> 00:08:36.750 align:middle line:90%
And there are these--

00:08:36.750 --> 00:08:38.460 align:middle line:84%
again, these
classic experiments,

00:08:38.460 --> 00:08:42.900 align:middle line:84%
like you bring people into a lab
and you have them play some game

00:08:42.900 --> 00:08:45.420 align:middle line:84%
and you say, as a
reward for doing this,

00:08:45.420 --> 00:08:49.740 align:middle line:84%
you get an MIT Department
of Economics water bottle.

00:08:49.740 --> 00:08:52.320 align:middle line:84%
And then you let them have
the MIT Economics water

00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:54.487 align:middle line:84%
bottle for a few minutes
and then say, oh, you know,

00:08:54.487 --> 00:08:57.150 align:middle line:90%
actually, I also have these--

00:08:57.150 --> 00:08:59.020 align:middle line:84%
I also have these
Lindt chocolate bars.

00:08:59.020 --> 00:09:02.640 align:middle line:84%
You can have the Lindt chocolate
bar instead of the water bottle,

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:05.350 align:middle line:84%
you know, if you want
to trade or whatever.

00:09:05.350 --> 00:09:08.140 align:middle line:84%
And then everyone says, no,
no, I'll keep the water bottle.

00:09:08.140 --> 00:09:09.520 align:middle line:84%
And then you run the
experiment the opposite way.

00:09:09.520 --> 00:09:11.062 align:middle line:84%
You give them the
Lindt chocolate bar

00:09:11.062 --> 00:09:14.280 align:middle line:84%
and say, oh, we also have this
MIT water bottle you could have

00:09:14.280 --> 00:09:14.805 align:middle line:90%
or whatever.

00:09:14.805 --> 00:09:16.930 align:middle line:84%
Nobody trades a chocolate
bar for the water bottle.

00:09:16.930 --> 00:09:19.470 align:middle line:84%
And so the idea
there is that how

00:09:19.470 --> 00:09:22.270 align:middle line:84%
do we explain that
people just get

00:09:22.270 --> 00:09:23.952 align:middle line:84%
attached to things
that they have.

00:09:23.952 --> 00:09:25.410 align:middle line:84%
The loss aversion,
what you've done

00:09:25.410 --> 00:09:28.850 align:middle line:84%
is you have two different
functions like this.

00:09:28.850 --> 00:09:32.440 align:middle line:84%
I have my water bottle
utility function

00:09:32.440 --> 00:09:35.220 align:middle line:84%
and my chocolate bar
utility function.

00:09:35.220 --> 00:09:36.970 align:middle line:84%
And once you've given
me the chocolate bar

00:09:36.970 --> 00:09:38.590 align:middle line:84%
and I've had it
for a few minutes,

00:09:38.590 --> 00:09:41.600 align:middle line:84%
then it feels like a loss
to give up the chocolate bar

00:09:41.600 --> 00:09:43.685 align:middle line:84%
and it would be a win
to get a water bottle.

00:09:43.685 --> 00:09:45.310 align:middle line:84%
But you know, a loss
of a chocolate bar

00:09:45.310 --> 00:09:47.393 align:middle line:84%
and a win to a water bottle,
that you wouldn't do,

00:09:47.393 --> 00:09:51.920 align:middle line:84%
and the same vice versa once
you've changed the endowments.

00:09:51.920 --> 00:09:53.270 align:middle line:90%
And I think this is--

00:09:53.270 --> 00:09:55.700 align:middle line:84%
people do think that this
is how you think about--

00:09:55.700 --> 00:09:58.220 align:middle line:84%
in some sense, this
is one explanation

00:09:58.220 --> 00:10:02.180 align:middle line:84%
for how stores try to
lure people in with

00:10:02.180 --> 00:10:04.220 align:middle line:84%
and then have high
last minute charges

00:10:04.220 --> 00:10:07.070 align:middle line:84%
or hidden charges is once
you've gotten used to the fact

00:10:07.070 --> 00:10:09.140 align:middle line:84%
that I'm going to
own this TV, it's

00:10:09.140 --> 00:10:12.020 align:middle line:84%
then hard to walk
away from the TV

00:10:12.020 --> 00:10:14.330 align:middle line:84%
because it's now a loss
of one in the TV dimension

00:10:14.330 --> 00:10:16.163 align:middle line:84%
because for the half
an hour you thought you

00:10:16.163 --> 00:10:18.830 align:middle line:90%
had that nice TV or something.

00:10:18.830 --> 00:10:21.110 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:10:21.110 --> 00:10:22.950 align:middle line:90%
Fairness preferences.

00:10:22.950 --> 00:10:26.480 align:middle line:84%
So obviously there's
nothing in utility function

00:10:26.480 --> 00:10:29.510 align:middle line:84%
that says that utility is
only based on your own wealth,

00:10:29.510 --> 00:10:31.710 align:middle line:84%
not on everybody else's
wealth in the entire world

00:10:31.710 --> 00:10:34.100 align:middle line:90%
and people can be altruistic.

00:10:34.100 --> 00:10:38.330 align:middle line:84%
But I think when
we're doing normal--

00:10:38.330 --> 00:10:40.130 align:middle line:84%
normally when we're
doing most topics,

00:10:40.130 --> 00:10:43.952 align:middle line:84%
we do stick with preferences
that are non-altruistic

00:10:43.952 --> 00:10:45.410 align:middle line:84%
and just everyone's
preferences are

00:10:45.410 --> 00:10:48.410 align:middle line:90%
based on their own consumption.

00:10:48.410 --> 00:10:50.540 align:middle line:84%
There's a number
of different models

00:10:50.540 --> 00:10:54.690 align:middle line:84%
out there of preferences that
do have some other regarding

00:10:54.690 --> 00:10:55.510 align:middle line:90%
nature to them.

00:10:55.510 --> 00:10:59.280 align:middle line:84%
So for instance, Fehr-Schmidt
is the most popular model

00:10:59.280 --> 00:11:01.590 align:middle line:84%
of fairness in the
behavioral literature.

00:11:01.590 --> 00:11:04.170 align:middle line:84%
What consumers do in
a Fehr-Schmidt model

00:11:04.170 --> 00:11:08.375 align:middle line:84%
is they are trying to
maximize their own payoff.

00:11:08.375 --> 00:11:10.500 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to write pi
here for the payoff rather

00:11:10.500 --> 00:11:11.620 align:middle line:90%
than the utility.

00:11:11.620 --> 00:11:14.190 align:middle line:84%
They're trying to
maximize their own payoff,

00:11:14.190 --> 00:11:16.980 align:middle line:84%
but they dislike when
other people are getting

00:11:16.980 --> 00:11:19.320 align:middle line:90%
more than them in a game.

00:11:19.320 --> 00:11:24.090 align:middle line:84%
And so we subtract
alpha times the fact

00:11:24.090 --> 00:11:26.430 align:middle line:84%
that the extent to which
other people's payoffs

00:11:26.430 --> 00:11:30.090 align:middle line:90%
exceed your payoff.

00:11:30.090 --> 00:11:33.180 align:middle line:84%
Fehr-Schmidt also
assume that consumers

00:11:33.180 --> 00:11:35.740 align:middle line:84%
dislike when other people
earn less than them.

00:11:35.740 --> 00:11:39.360 align:middle line:84%
They are other-regarding,
and so they put weight beta

00:11:39.360 --> 00:11:43.470 align:middle line:84%
on getting more money than other
people in the game are getting.

00:11:43.470 --> 00:11:47.370 align:middle line:84%
But Fehr-Schmidt assume
that most people have

00:11:47.370 --> 00:11:49.620 align:middle line:84%
a sense of fairness, but
the fairness is more acute

00:11:49.620 --> 00:11:52.210 align:middle line:84%
when they're the ones
being treated unfairly,

00:11:52.210 --> 00:11:53.940 align:middle line:84%
and so alpha is
bigger than beta.

00:11:53.940 --> 00:11:57.870 align:middle line:84%
Then you put more weight on
me getting the small share

00:11:57.870 --> 00:12:00.810 align:middle line:84%
than on other people getting
a small share, although I

00:12:00.810 --> 00:12:02.760 align:middle line:90%
don't like that either.

00:12:02.760 --> 00:12:06.960 align:middle line:84%
And then again, you start
with any utility model,

00:12:06.960 --> 00:12:08.820 align:middle line:84%
and then you could do
the Fehr-Schmidt model

00:12:08.820 --> 00:12:11.400 align:middle line:84%
with two extra parameters
alpha and beta,

00:12:11.400 --> 00:12:12.960 align:middle line:84%
and then you can
think about what

00:12:12.960 --> 00:12:15.640 align:middle line:84%
happens in that model as a
function of alpha and beta.

00:12:15.640 --> 00:12:18.280 align:middle line:84%
So in some sense it becomes
just a plug and play thing.

00:12:18.280 --> 00:12:24.413 align:middle line:84%
Take a standard model, put in
the alpha beta preferences,

00:12:24.413 --> 00:12:25.830 align:middle line:84%
and then you can
resolve the model

00:12:25.830 --> 00:12:28.038 align:middle line:84%
and see what happens as a
function of alpha and beta.

00:12:28.038 --> 00:12:31.485 align:middle line:84%
And you can calibrate alpha
and beta to what's in the data.

00:12:31.485 --> 00:12:35.125 align:middle line:90%


00:12:35.125 --> 00:12:38.470 align:middle line:84%
Most of the examples I
picked out to do today in IO,

00:12:38.470 --> 00:12:41.600 align:middle line:84%
I'm doing what are called
quasi-hyperbolic preferences.

00:12:41.600 --> 00:12:45.190 align:middle line:84%
So in preferences with
quasi-hyperbolic discounting,

00:12:45.190 --> 00:12:48.700 align:middle line:84%
consumers are maximizing,
at each point in time,

00:12:48.700 --> 00:12:52.030 align:middle line:84%
the utility of the
consumption they get today,

00:12:52.030 --> 00:12:57.980 align:middle line:84%
plus beta times a discounted
sum of future preferences.

00:12:57.980 --> 00:13:00.970 align:middle line:84%
So everyone always
cares about today

00:13:00.970 --> 00:13:03.590 align:middle line:84%
more than they care
about all future periods,

00:13:03.590 --> 00:13:07.930 align:middle line:84%
and so your trade-off
between should I

00:13:07.930 --> 00:13:10.778 align:middle line:84%
clean up my apartment 11 days
from now or 12 days from now,

00:13:10.778 --> 00:13:12.820 align:middle line:84%
you're completely indifferent
between whether you

00:13:12.820 --> 00:13:15.770 align:middle line:84%
spend a day cleaning up 11 days
from now or 12 days from now.

00:13:15.770 --> 00:13:18.140 align:middle line:84%
But should I do it
today versus tomorrow?

00:13:18.140 --> 00:13:20.270 align:middle line:84%
You really want to put
it off to tomorrow?

00:13:20.270 --> 00:13:23.650 align:middle line:84%
So there's some much
bigger emphasis discounting

00:13:23.650 --> 00:13:25.930 align:middle line:84%
between today and
all future times

00:13:25.930 --> 00:13:29.930 align:middle line:84%
than there is between future
times that come later.

00:13:29.930 --> 00:13:32.740 align:middle line:84%
So the discounting between
any two future periods

00:13:32.740 --> 00:13:37.470 align:middle line:84%
is delta, but between
the current period and--

00:13:37.470 --> 00:13:41.200 align:middle line:84%
you put weight one on today,
beta delta on tomorrow,

00:13:41.200 --> 00:13:43.700 align:middle line:84%
beta delta squared
on the next day

00:13:43.700 --> 00:13:49.210 align:middle line:84%
so that the ratio is beta
delta here and only delta here.

00:13:49.210 --> 00:13:51.050 align:middle line:84%
Again, so people
have preferences.

00:13:51.050 --> 00:13:52.570 align:middle line:84%
Like I would like
to go on a diet,

00:13:52.570 --> 00:13:55.210 align:middle line:84%
but I would like to go on
a diet starting tomorrow.

00:13:55.210 --> 00:13:56.710 align:middle line:84%
Or I would like to
save more money,

00:13:56.710 --> 00:14:00.880 align:middle line:84%
but I'd like to save more money
after I buy this one TV I really

00:14:00.880 --> 00:14:03.040 align:middle line:84%
like and then I'm going
to start saving my money,

00:14:03.040 --> 00:14:04.960 align:middle line:90%
or something like that.

00:14:04.960 --> 00:14:09.190 align:middle line:84%
And then there are
variants of this model.

00:14:09.190 --> 00:14:12.370 align:middle line:84%
Sophisticated
consumers recognize--

00:14:12.370 --> 00:14:14.950 align:middle line:84%
sophisticated hyperbolic
consumers recognize

00:14:14.950 --> 00:14:18.580 align:middle line:84%
that they have these
preferences and they

00:14:18.580 --> 00:14:22.210 align:middle line:84%
want to do things to commit
themselves to not behave

00:14:22.210 --> 00:14:24.070 align:middle line:84%
in a hyperbolic
way, or they know

00:14:24.070 --> 00:14:26.480 align:middle line:84%
that they need to commit
their future selves to things

00:14:26.480 --> 00:14:28.000 align:middle line:84%
so their future selves
will actually do what they

00:14:28.000 --> 00:14:29.530 align:middle line:90%
want their future selves to do.

00:14:29.530 --> 00:14:32.510 align:middle line:84%
So it's like, I am
going to put something

00:14:32.510 --> 00:14:35.530 align:middle line:84%
on my phone that shuts me off
of social media for some number

00:14:35.530 --> 00:14:38.560 align:middle line:84%
of hours to commit that
future lazy self of mine

00:14:38.560 --> 00:14:41.860 align:middle line:84%
to actually get work
done and not spend time

00:14:41.860 --> 00:14:44.680 align:middle line:90%
scrolling through social media.

00:14:44.680 --> 00:14:46.860 align:middle line:90%
The other ones are--

00:14:46.860 --> 00:14:49.000 align:middle line:90%
other models, the naive model.

00:14:49.000 --> 00:14:51.645 align:middle line:84%
The naive model,
you're hyperbolic

00:14:51.645 --> 00:14:53.770 align:middle line:84%
and you don't recognize
that your future selves are

00:14:53.770 --> 00:14:57.370 align:middle line:84%
hyperbolic and you just
think, I have willpower.

00:14:57.370 --> 00:14:58.580 align:middle line:90%
These are my preferences.

00:14:58.580 --> 00:14:59.950 align:middle line:84%
It's perfectly
reasonable for me to say

00:14:59.950 --> 00:15:02.200 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to sit here and
scroll through social media,

00:15:02.200 --> 00:15:05.210 align:middle line:84%
and then tomorrow my future
self will not scroll.

00:15:05.210 --> 00:15:06.940 align:middle line:84%
I'll just make the
plan that this week I

00:15:06.940 --> 00:15:08.350 align:middle line:90%
get six hours of social media.

00:15:08.350 --> 00:15:10.030 align:middle line:84%
I'll use it all
up this afternoon,

00:15:10.030 --> 00:15:13.310 align:middle line:84%
and then my future self will not
do any social media tomorrow.

00:15:13.310 --> 00:15:16.540 align:middle line:84%
And that's my plan
for this week.

00:15:16.540 --> 00:15:21.430 align:middle line:84%
And then the
partially naive model,

00:15:21.430 --> 00:15:23.470 align:middle line:84%
an extension to go
from a two parameter

00:15:23.470 --> 00:15:26.380 align:middle line:84%
to a three parameter
hyperbolic extension

00:15:26.380 --> 00:15:29.810 align:middle line:90%
is I am a beta delta consumer.

00:15:29.810 --> 00:15:34.180 align:middle line:84%
I recognize that my future
self is also hyperbolic,

00:15:34.180 --> 00:15:37.540 align:middle line:84%
but I think my future self
is like a beta hat delta

00:15:37.540 --> 00:15:40.550 align:middle line:84%
consumer with beta
hat greater than beta.

00:15:40.550 --> 00:15:43.545 align:middle line:90%
So I just underestimate.

00:15:43.545 --> 00:15:47.140 align:middle line:84%
I recognize that I'm
hyperbolic, but I

00:15:47.140 --> 00:15:49.810 align:middle line:84%
underestimate the degree to
which my future self is hyper

00:15:49.810 --> 00:15:52.531 align:middle line:90%
hyperbolic.

00:15:52.531 --> 00:15:54.325 align:middle line:84%
And again, if you
think about the delta

00:15:54.325 --> 00:15:56.200 align:middle line:84%
as already being a
parameter we already have,

00:15:56.200 --> 00:15:58.690 align:middle line:84%
again, maybe just
beta and beta hat

00:15:58.690 --> 00:16:01.620 align:middle line:84%
are two parameters you add to
the standard rational model.

00:16:01.620 --> 00:16:04.750 align:middle line:90%


00:16:04.750 --> 00:16:06.160 align:middle line:90%
Other common examples.

00:16:06.160 --> 00:16:08.950 align:middle line:84%
I won't talk about IO
examples today with them.

00:16:08.950 --> 00:16:12.400 align:middle line:90%
Imperfect Bayesian updating.

00:16:12.400 --> 00:16:14.095 align:middle line:84%
Eyster and Rabin
proposed this model.

00:16:14.095 --> 00:16:15.970 align:middle line:84%
There's a lot of evidence
that people are not

00:16:15.970 --> 00:16:17.390 align:middle line:90%
so good at Bayesian updating.

00:16:17.390 --> 00:16:19.640 align:middle line:84%
They propose this model of
imperfect Bayesian updating

00:16:19.640 --> 00:16:22.780 align:middle line:84%
that's just the simplest
one in the world.

00:16:22.780 --> 00:16:26.410 align:middle line:84%
Your posterior on
theta after observing

00:16:26.410 --> 00:16:30.910 align:middle line:84%
some signal x is some constant
times your prior plus 1

00:16:30.910 --> 00:16:37.040 align:middle line:84%
minus chi times what the correct
preference is, correct Bayesian

00:16:37.040 --> 00:16:37.540 align:middle line:90%
updating is.

00:16:37.540 --> 00:16:42.070 align:middle line:84%
So people just under-update
when they get new information.

00:16:42.070 --> 00:16:43.913 align:middle line:84%
They tend to be
overly confident,

00:16:43.913 --> 00:16:44.913 align:middle line:90%
stick with their priors.

00:16:44.913 --> 00:16:48.040 align:middle line:90%


00:16:48.040 --> 00:16:50.440 align:middle line:90%
Overconfidence bias.

00:16:50.440 --> 00:16:53.110 align:middle line:84%
Overconfidence bias
is people think

00:16:53.110 --> 00:16:55.750 align:middle line:84%
they are better than they
are at many, many things.

00:16:55.750 --> 00:17:01.720 align:middle line:84%
A classic example is ask a class
how many miles is it from Boston

00:17:01.720 --> 00:17:07.105 align:middle line:84%
to Buenos Aires and write down
your best guess for Boston

00:17:07.105 --> 00:17:08.359 align:middle line:90%
to Buenos Aires.

00:17:08.359 --> 00:17:11.349 align:middle line:84%
And now write down a
50% confidence interval

00:17:11.349 --> 00:17:12.950 align:middle line:90%
or a 90% confidence interval.

00:17:12.950 --> 00:17:15.208 align:middle line:90%


00:17:15.208 --> 00:17:17.500 align:middle line:84%
You admit you don't know how
far it is to Buenos Aires,

00:17:17.500 --> 00:17:21.430 align:middle line:84%
but write down an interval
you think there's a 50% chance

00:17:21.430 --> 00:17:23.470 align:middle line:84%
that the distance from
Boston to Buenos Aires

00:17:23.470 --> 00:17:25.339 align:middle line:84%
is within the interval
you write down.

00:17:25.339 --> 00:17:28.150 align:middle line:84%
And overwhelmingly, I
don't know, is it, like,

00:17:28.150 --> 00:17:29.530 align:middle line:90%
50% of the people--

00:17:29.530 --> 00:17:31.780 align:middle line:84%
like if you ask for 90%
confidence interval,

00:17:31.780 --> 00:17:34.300 align:middle line:84%
50% of the correct answers
are within the 90%--

00:17:34.300 --> 00:17:37.120 align:middle line:84%
50% of the time, the
90% confidence interval

00:17:37.120 --> 00:17:38.380 align:middle line:90%
contains the answer.

00:17:38.380 --> 00:17:40.810 align:middle line:84%
30% of time, the 50%
confidence interval

00:17:40.810 --> 00:17:42.700 align:middle line:90%
contains the correct answer.

00:17:42.700 --> 00:17:45.580 align:middle line:84%
It's just people think they
are better at many things

00:17:45.580 --> 00:17:48.490 align:middle line:90%
than they are.

00:17:48.490 --> 00:17:50.270 align:middle line:90%
Self-serving bias.

00:17:50.270 --> 00:17:52.982 align:middle line:84%
This is the idea that
we all like to think--

00:17:52.982 --> 00:17:54.940 align:middle line:84%
this is that we all think
we're good at things.

00:17:54.940 --> 00:17:57.740 align:middle line:84%
Self-serving bias is we all like
to think we're good at things.

00:17:57.740 --> 00:18:00.520 align:middle line:84%
So you make a
decision, and then when

00:18:00.520 --> 00:18:02.740 align:middle line:84%
you're called on to
make a second decision,

00:18:02.740 --> 00:18:05.110 align:middle line:84%
you make a second decision
in a way that makes

00:18:05.110 --> 00:18:08.470 align:middle line:90%
your first decision correct.

00:18:08.470 --> 00:18:11.020 align:middle line:84%
That is so instead
of like admitting--

00:18:11.020 --> 00:18:14.670 align:middle line:90%


00:18:14.670 --> 00:18:15.532 align:middle line:90%
you make some plan.

00:18:15.532 --> 00:18:16.740 align:middle line:90%
You're going to go somewhere.

00:18:16.740 --> 00:18:20.188 align:middle line:84%
And then you get there
and realize, wow,

00:18:20.188 --> 00:18:22.480 align:middle line:84%
I spent half an hour on the
train to get to this place.

00:18:22.480 --> 00:18:25.080 align:middle line:84%
This restaurant looks way
more expensive than I wanted

00:18:25.080 --> 00:18:26.880 align:middle line:90%
or I don't want to be here.

00:18:26.880 --> 00:18:27.990 align:middle line:90%
Food looks bad.

00:18:27.990 --> 00:18:31.290 align:middle line:84%
But once you're there, you go
ahead and eat there anyway,

00:18:31.290 --> 00:18:34.800 align:middle line:84%
because if you don't
eat there, you're

00:18:34.800 --> 00:18:37.650 align:middle line:84%
admitting that I made a
bad decision in deciding

00:18:37.650 --> 00:18:40.150 align:middle line:84%
to pick out this restaurant
and come here for half an hour.

00:18:40.150 --> 00:18:42.317 align:middle line:84%
And then you want to make
my first decision correct.

00:18:42.317 --> 00:18:44.192 align:middle line:84%
The only way my first
decision can be correct

00:18:44.192 --> 00:18:45.930 align:middle line:84%
is if I then carry
out my second decision

00:18:45.930 --> 00:18:47.573 align:middle line:90%
to go ahead and eat there.

00:18:47.573 --> 00:18:48.990 align:middle line:84%
Then maybe you
don't like the food

00:18:48.990 --> 00:18:52.140 align:middle line:84%
and you're like, oh, wow that
was unknowable, so I just

00:18:52.140 --> 00:18:53.650 align:middle line:90%
had a bad draw or whatever.

00:18:53.650 --> 00:18:55.540 align:middle line:84%
But it wasn't an
ex-ante bad decision.

00:18:55.540 --> 00:18:58.092 align:middle line:84%
But the idea of
self-serving is we always--

00:18:58.092 --> 00:18:59.550 align:middle line:84%
we follow these
paths in life where

00:18:59.550 --> 00:19:04.020 align:middle line:84%
we just keep taking decisions,
and each step we take

00:19:04.020 --> 00:19:06.720 align:middle line:84%
a next step that's consistent
with the earlier steps

00:19:06.720 --> 00:19:09.540 align:middle line:84%
being right, rather than
admitting your first step

00:19:09.540 --> 00:19:12.195 align:middle line:84%
was wrong and I should have
just admitted my first step was

00:19:12.195 --> 00:19:14.820 align:middle line:84%
wrong and said, yeah, I made a
bad decision there and gone back

00:19:14.820 --> 00:19:18.060 align:middle line:90%
and start over or something.

00:19:18.060 --> 00:19:21.790 align:middle line:84%
OK, anyway, so
many, many things.

00:19:21.790 --> 00:19:24.720 align:middle line:84%
And in some ways,
I think this is a--

00:19:24.720 --> 00:19:26.850 align:middle line:84%
field has expanded
enough in the last decade

00:19:26.850 --> 00:19:30.720 align:middle line:84%
that it's no longer as
true, but in some ways,

00:19:30.720 --> 00:19:33.390 align:middle line:84%
once you have this view
of-- and this is sometimes

00:19:33.390 --> 00:19:35.790 align:middle line:84%
why behavioral
economics does so well

00:19:35.790 --> 00:19:39.420 align:middle line:84%
in every field of economics,
because if you think

00:19:39.420 --> 00:19:41.790 align:middle line:84%
about it, before behavioral
came into fashion,

00:19:41.790 --> 00:19:43.560 align:middle line:84%
what had Tirole done
in his textbook?

00:19:43.560 --> 00:19:46.320 align:middle line:84%
He worked out with
rational consumers what

00:19:46.320 --> 00:19:48.607 align:middle line:84%
happens with monopoly pricing
and price discrimination

00:19:48.607 --> 00:19:50.940 align:middle line:84%
and selling durable goods and
horizontal differentiation

00:19:50.940 --> 00:19:53.592 align:middle line:84%
and vertical differentiation
and search costs and so on.

00:19:53.592 --> 00:19:55.800 align:middle line:84%
You basically take the entire
syllabus of this course

00:19:55.800 --> 00:19:58.590 align:middle line:84%
and it's all rational
consumers and firms

00:19:58.590 --> 00:20:00.570 align:middle line:90%
dealing with this situation.

00:20:00.570 --> 00:20:03.630 align:middle line:84%
And then if you think about
from the behavioral perspective,

00:20:03.630 --> 00:20:07.810 align:middle line:84%
well, rationality is just one
incorrect model of consumers,

00:20:07.810 --> 00:20:12.360 align:middle line:84%
and there are actually
this many or more

00:20:12.360 --> 00:20:13.920 align:middle line:84%
other models of
consumers that would

00:20:13.920 --> 00:20:15.670 align:middle line:90%
be better models of consumers.

00:20:15.670 --> 00:20:17.190 align:middle line:84%
In some sense,
what we need to do

00:20:17.190 --> 00:20:22.390 align:middle line:84%
is take the entire field of IO
as it existed in the year 2005

00:20:22.390 --> 00:20:24.972 align:middle line:84%
and multiply it
out into a matrix.

00:20:24.972 --> 00:20:26.680 align:middle line:84%
What happens in every
one of these models

00:20:26.680 --> 00:20:30.755 align:middle line:84%
with quasi-hyperbolic consumers,
with loss averse consumers,

00:20:30.755 --> 00:20:32.380 align:middle line:84%
with consumers who
care about fairness,

00:20:32.380 --> 00:20:35.680 align:middle line:84%
with overconfident
consumers, and so on?

00:20:35.680 --> 00:20:39.020 align:middle line:84%
It seems like, how can I
think of a paper to write?

00:20:39.020 --> 00:20:41.410 align:middle line:84%
But in some sense, you
can take every paper that

00:20:41.410 --> 00:20:45.850 align:middle line:84%
exists in the literature and
write 14 new papers about it,

00:20:45.850 --> 00:20:48.070 align:middle line:84%
and so it just opens
up this massive field.

00:20:48.070 --> 00:20:52.840 align:middle line:84%
And again, is--
what do I have here?

00:20:52.840 --> 00:20:54.700 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:20:54.700 --> 00:20:57.050 align:middle line:84%
Are all of those
good papers to write?

00:20:57.050 --> 00:20:58.540 align:middle line:90%
You know, probably not.

00:20:58.540 --> 00:21:04.160 align:middle line:84%
It may be that maybe you try to
do horizontal differentiation,

00:21:04.160 --> 00:21:05.813 align:middle line:84%
you put in
quasi-hyperbolic consumers

00:21:05.813 --> 00:21:07.480 align:middle line:84%
and then you realize,
wait, the hoteling

00:21:07.480 --> 00:21:09.080 align:middle line:90%
model is a static model.

00:21:09.080 --> 00:21:10.400 align:middle line:90%
It's all in one period.

00:21:10.400 --> 00:21:13.900 align:middle line:84%
Therefore, hyperbolic has
nothing to do with it.

00:21:13.900 --> 00:21:17.920 align:middle line:84%
Certainly loss averse consumers
in a horizontal differentiation

00:21:17.920 --> 00:21:20.350 align:middle line:84%
model matter and consumers
could care about fairness

00:21:20.350 --> 00:21:24.310 align:middle line:84%
and consumers could be
overconfident in their thoughts

00:21:24.310 --> 00:21:27.340 align:middle line:84%
about their own
preferences or whatever.

00:21:27.340 --> 00:21:29.710 align:middle line:84%
But in some ways,
there are many,

00:21:29.710 --> 00:21:32.510 align:middle line:84%
many of these boxes that
have something there,

00:21:32.510 --> 00:21:34.360 align:middle line:84%
and in some ways,
I think what you

00:21:34.360 --> 00:21:38.170 align:middle line:84%
want is there's this opportunity
to explore them and think

00:21:38.170 --> 00:21:43.810 align:middle line:84%
about which of these preferences
might be relevant to which

00:21:43.810 --> 00:21:47.230 align:middle line:84%
of these models we looked at
and think about what is there

00:21:47.230 --> 00:21:48.500 align:middle line:90%
interesting to say.

00:21:48.500 --> 00:21:51.490 align:middle line:84%
And I think what a lot of
what people are interested in

00:21:51.490 --> 00:21:55.330 align:middle line:84%
is if you can tell stories
where I make some small change

00:21:55.330 --> 00:21:57.370 align:middle line:84%
to the existing
model and now I get

00:21:57.370 --> 00:22:00.640 align:middle line:84%
a big change in the
outcomes, so I don't

00:22:00.640 --> 00:22:02.828 align:middle line:90%
need an enormous irrationality.

00:22:02.828 --> 00:22:04.870 align:middle line:84%
In some sense, the original
model we started with

00:22:04.870 --> 00:22:06.420 align:middle line:84%
was very sensitive
to rationality.

00:22:06.420 --> 00:22:08.962 align:middle line:84%
When you put in a small change,
it makes a big difference.

00:22:08.962 --> 00:22:11.170 align:middle line:84%
And you could certainly
imagine many of these things,

00:22:11.170 --> 00:22:14.230 align:middle line:84%
like, it could make a big
difference in durable goods

00:22:14.230 --> 00:22:18.550 align:middle line:84%
models if we had
quasi-hyperbolic preferences.

00:22:18.550 --> 00:22:19.220 align:middle line:90%
Take an example.

00:22:19.220 --> 00:22:20.803 align:middle line:84%
I guess you probably
didn't get there,

00:22:20.803 --> 00:22:27.800 align:middle line:84%
but when I talked about my
add-on pricing paper about

00:22:27.800 --> 00:22:32.030 align:middle line:84%
do firms want to post
an unadvertised-- post

00:22:32.030 --> 00:22:35.430 align:middle line:84%
an advertised price that
doesn't include some add-on

00:22:35.430 --> 00:22:37.430 align:middle line:84%
and then hold up consumers
for the full monopoly

00:22:37.430 --> 00:22:40.847 align:middle line:84%
price of the add-on after
consumers show up at the firm.

00:22:40.847 --> 00:22:42.680 align:middle line:84%
Back in the back half
of that paper, there's

00:22:42.680 --> 00:22:45.890 align:middle line:84%
a section that says in a
game where the two firms are

00:22:45.890 --> 00:22:49.670 align:middle line:84%
simultaneously deciding whether
to advertise the base good only

00:22:49.670 --> 00:22:52.285 align:middle line:84%
or the base good and an
add-on, which would they do?

00:22:52.285 --> 00:22:53.660 align:middle line:84%
And the answer is
the firms don't

00:22:53.660 --> 00:22:55.785 align:middle line:84%
want-- it's not individually
rational in that model

00:22:55.785 --> 00:22:58.310 align:middle line:84%
for the firms to
do add-on pricing.

00:22:58.310 --> 00:23:02.095 align:middle line:84%
However, if you add a small
number of behavioral consumers

00:23:02.095 --> 00:23:04.220 align:middle line:84%
who don't realize how
expensive the add-on is going

00:23:04.220 --> 00:23:06.050 align:middle line:84%
to be until they get
to the point of sale,

00:23:06.050 --> 00:23:07.530 align:middle line:90%
then it can make sense.

00:23:07.530 --> 00:23:10.430 align:middle line:84%
And so it's just an example
where you have a small change

00:23:10.430 --> 00:23:11.930 align:middle line:84%
to the model, like
a small fraction

00:23:11.930 --> 00:23:17.841 align:middle line:84%
of the irrational consumers can
have a big change on the model.

00:23:17.841 --> 00:23:23.780 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to start with
just, you know, very early paper

00:23:23.780 --> 00:23:25.710 align:middle line:90%
bringing out some basic ideas.

00:23:25.710 --> 00:23:29.630 align:middle line:84%
So this is Della
Vigna and Malmendier

00:23:29.630 --> 00:23:33.530 align:middle line:84%
thinking about how a monopolist
would design products or sales

00:23:33.530 --> 00:23:37.430 align:middle line:84%
mechanisms to exploit
hyperbolic consumers.

00:23:37.430 --> 00:23:41.000 align:middle line:84%
And this is a multi-stage
game motivated by health clubs

00:23:41.000 --> 00:23:46.150 align:middle line:84%
where the idea is that everybody
wishes they went to the gym more

00:23:46.150 --> 00:23:49.240 align:middle line:90%
than they actually do.

00:23:49.240 --> 00:23:51.170 align:middle line:90%
And so here's their model.

00:23:51.170 --> 00:23:54.470 align:middle line:84%
So firms offer consumers
a two part tariff.

00:23:54.470 --> 00:23:57.730 align:middle line:84%
You pay l, which is a
sign-up fee to join the gym.

00:23:57.730 --> 00:24:01.400 align:middle line:84%
And then if you visit x
times, you pay p times x.

00:24:01.400 --> 00:24:04.930 align:middle line:84%
So every time you come to the
gym, you pay p to go there.

00:24:04.930 --> 00:24:07.870 align:middle line:84%
Consumers have these beta
delta hyperbolic preferences

00:24:07.870 --> 00:24:10.660 align:middle line:84%
and they say yes or no, I
will pay my upfront fee l

00:24:10.660 --> 00:24:12.670 align:middle line:90%
to join the gym.

00:24:12.670 --> 00:24:16.335 align:middle line:84%
And then in their model
there's uncertainty.

00:24:16.335 --> 00:24:19.100 align:middle line:84%
You know you should join the
gym and that it's good for you

00:24:19.100 --> 00:24:20.830 align:middle line:84%
and you don't remember
how unpleasant

00:24:20.830 --> 00:24:22.218 align:middle line:90%
it is to go to the gym.

00:24:22.218 --> 00:24:24.010 align:middle line:84%
Like, how long does it
take to actually get

00:24:24.010 --> 00:24:29.230 align:middle line:84%
there, how unpleasant
is the exercising there,

00:24:29.230 --> 00:24:31.540 align:middle line:84%
how gross is the
showers at the gym,

00:24:31.540 --> 00:24:33.580 align:middle line:84%
and whatever, how
much are you going

00:24:33.580 --> 00:24:36.250 align:middle line:84%
to dislike exercising
once you get there.

00:24:36.250 --> 00:24:38.470 align:middle line:84%
And so after you
go to the gym, you

00:24:38.470 --> 00:24:41.710 align:middle line:84%
learn your disutility
d that you incur

00:24:41.710 --> 00:24:44.540 align:middle line:90%
every time you go to the gym.

00:24:44.540 --> 00:24:48.440 align:middle line:84%
And then you then choose
how many times am I going

00:24:48.440 --> 00:24:49.700 align:middle line:90%
to go to the gym this month.

00:24:49.700 --> 00:24:53.180 align:middle line:84%
To simplify the algebra,
they do either zero or one,

00:24:53.180 --> 00:24:55.823 align:middle line:84%
but obviously we're
thinking of one

00:24:55.823 --> 00:24:58.490 align:middle line:84%
as being go to the gym every day
and zero as never go to the gym

00:24:58.490 --> 00:24:59.600 align:middle line:90%
or something like that.

00:24:59.600 --> 00:25:03.590 align:middle line:84%
And then you incur
disutility d plus p times x

00:25:03.590 --> 00:25:05.780 align:middle line:90%
when you go to the gym.

00:25:05.780 --> 00:25:11.120 align:middle line:84%
And then you get a delayed
health benefit x times b.

00:25:11.120 --> 00:25:13.460 align:middle line:84%
So you do feel better after
you've been to the gym.

00:25:13.460 --> 00:25:16.910 align:middle line:84%
You're happy that you went
yesterday, and that's x times b.

00:25:16.910 --> 00:25:17.480 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:25:17.480 --> 00:25:20.780 align:middle line:84%
And so the hyperbolic
preferences come in here

00:25:20.780 --> 00:25:27.020 align:middle line:84%
where the consumer at t
equals one who's hyperbolic

00:25:27.020 --> 00:25:29.390 align:middle line:84%
wants his future
selves to go to the gym

00:25:29.390 --> 00:25:35.000 align:middle line:84%
because he treats the disutility
and the benefit as both having

00:25:35.000 --> 00:25:37.552 align:middle line:84%
roughly equal weight because
they're just both future things,

00:25:37.552 --> 00:25:39.260 align:middle line:84%
and he thinks the
future benefit is worth

00:25:39.260 --> 00:25:42.200 align:middle line:90%
the future disutility, probably.

00:25:42.200 --> 00:25:45.170 align:middle line:84%
But then the consumer
who's actually here making

00:25:45.170 --> 00:25:47.480 align:middle line:84%
the do I go to the gym
today or not decision,

00:25:47.480 --> 00:25:50.420 align:middle line:84%
that consumer is
treating the disutility

00:25:50.420 --> 00:25:53.510 align:middle line:84%
and the money paid as a present
thing and the health benefit

00:25:53.510 --> 00:25:56.930 align:middle line:84%
as a future thing, so that
lazy future self is tempted

00:25:56.930 --> 00:26:00.080 align:middle line:84%
to not go today and
say, OK, the tomorrow

00:26:00.080 --> 00:26:02.650 align:middle line:84%
self will be the one to go to
the gym, not the today self.

00:26:02.650 --> 00:26:05.720 align:middle line:90%


00:26:05.720 --> 00:26:10.700 align:middle line:84%
So what are some of
the observations?

00:26:10.700 --> 00:26:15.620 align:middle line:84%
First monopoly observation
is that in this model,

00:26:15.620 --> 00:26:22.310 align:middle line:84%
we get distorted pricing
where p is distorted down.

00:26:22.310 --> 00:26:25.610 align:middle line:84%
In their paper, they have
a costs, marginal costs,

00:26:25.610 --> 00:26:27.570 align:middle line:84%
c times x of having
people go to the gym.

00:26:27.570 --> 00:26:30.000 align:middle line:84%
I mean, c may be small,
but the gym does--

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:31.610 align:middle line:90%
they have to wash your towel.

00:26:31.610 --> 00:26:33.090 align:middle line:84%
They may wipe down
the equipment.

00:26:33.090 --> 00:26:35.690 align:middle line:84%
They have some capacity levels,
so they incur some small costs

00:26:35.690 --> 00:26:36.950 align:middle line:90%
c when you come.

00:26:36.950 --> 00:26:42.980 align:middle line:84%
But so the main distortion we
get is we get p less than c.

00:26:42.980 --> 00:26:45.810 align:middle line:84%
This can be going
to the gym is free.

00:26:45.810 --> 00:26:48.060 align:middle line:84%
You don't pay when you show
up on your gym membership.

00:26:48.060 --> 00:26:51.260 align:middle line:84%
Or it could be that p is even
negative, that you don't pay

00:26:51.260 --> 00:26:53.570 align:middle line:84%
and they give you a free
juice or they give you

00:26:53.570 --> 00:26:56.570 align:middle line:84%
some other things for free
that they don't charge you

00:26:56.570 --> 00:27:00.720 align:middle line:90%
for when you're at the gym.

00:27:00.720 --> 00:27:01.290 align:middle line:90%
Why?

00:27:01.290 --> 00:27:02.800 align:middle line:90%
Why do they distort p down?

00:27:02.800 --> 00:27:04.100 align:middle line:90%
There are two reasons.

00:27:04.100 --> 00:27:07.470 align:middle line:84%
First is let's think about the
sophisticated hyperbolic model

00:27:07.470 --> 00:27:10.770 align:middle line:84%
where this consumer knows
that his future self is

00:27:10.770 --> 00:27:12.810 align:middle line:90%
going to be lazy.

00:27:12.810 --> 00:27:15.660 align:middle line:84%
So that means he knows there's
going to be a problem of,

00:27:15.660 --> 00:27:17.080 align:middle line:84%
I sign up for the
gym membership.

00:27:17.080 --> 00:27:20.910 align:middle line:84%
This guy is not going to
go, and therefore, I'm

00:27:20.910 --> 00:27:22.650 align:middle line:84%
not going to get
the health benefit.

00:27:22.650 --> 00:27:25.500 align:middle line:84%
So what happens is the
sophisticated consumer

00:27:25.500 --> 00:27:29.890 align:middle line:84%
wants to commit his future
self to actually go to the gym.

00:27:29.890 --> 00:27:32.740 align:middle line:84%
And how does he commit his
future self to go to the gym?

00:27:32.740 --> 00:27:36.720 align:middle line:90%
He makes the price.

00:27:36.720 --> 00:27:39.240 align:middle line:84%
If you offer a contract
where the p is negative--

00:27:39.240 --> 00:27:41.970 align:middle line:84%
there's all kinds of free stuff
offered at the gym and they

00:27:41.970 --> 00:27:43.350 align:middle line:90%
don't charge you any money--

00:27:43.350 --> 00:27:45.727 align:middle line:84%
then you're thinking, that
lazy future self of mine

00:27:45.727 --> 00:27:47.310 align:middle line:84%
is going to go to
the gym because he's

00:27:47.310 --> 00:27:50.700 align:middle line:84%
going to want to get the
negative $10 fee for going

00:27:50.700 --> 00:27:52.710 align:middle line:84%
to the gym, and
then that's going

00:27:52.710 --> 00:27:56.490 align:middle line:84%
to make him willing to incur the
disutility to get the benefit.

00:27:56.490 --> 00:27:59.280 align:middle line:84%
And so the
sophisticated consumers

00:27:59.280 --> 00:28:02.320 align:middle line:84%
want to have a
contract-- and they're

00:28:02.320 --> 00:28:04.510 align:middle line:84%
willing to pay a lot
of money up front

00:28:04.510 --> 00:28:07.180 align:middle line:84%
to get the contract
with the low p that's

00:28:07.180 --> 00:28:10.400 align:middle line:84%
going to force their future
self to go to the gym.

00:28:10.400 --> 00:28:15.290 align:middle line:84%
So that's the
sophisticated argument.

00:28:15.290 --> 00:28:17.330 align:middle line:84%
If consumers are
naive hyperbolics

00:28:17.330 --> 00:28:19.430 align:middle line:84%
instead of sophisticated
hyperbolics,

00:28:19.430 --> 00:28:21.650 align:middle line:90%
you also want them--

00:28:21.650 --> 00:28:24.170 align:middle line:84%
you also want to
do p less than c.

00:28:24.170 --> 00:28:28.280 align:middle line:84%
And the idea here is that the
naive hyperbolic consumers

00:28:28.280 --> 00:28:30.610 align:middle line:84%
misestimate how often
they go to the gym.

00:28:30.610 --> 00:28:35.540 align:middle line:84%
They say, OK, you know, I know
b is bigger than d, therefore,

00:28:35.540 --> 00:28:39.417 align:middle line:84%
if b is bigger-- if I know b
is bigger than d and the price

00:28:39.417 --> 00:28:41.000 align:middle line:84%
is zero, my future
self is going to go

00:28:41.000 --> 00:28:42.970 align:middle line:90%
to the gym every single day.

00:28:42.970 --> 00:28:45.470 align:middle line:84%
And then what you can say is,
so if you have this person who

00:28:45.470 --> 00:28:50.090 align:middle line:84%
thinks they're going to go
every-- so suppose that we have

00:28:50.090 --> 00:28:54.530 align:middle line:84%
the benefit b is bigger
than the disutility d,

00:28:54.530 --> 00:28:58.800 align:middle line:84%
but the benefit b is less
than beta times delta.

00:28:58.800 --> 00:28:59.300 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:28:59.300 --> 00:29:06.010 align:middle line:84%
So this is the case where
the future self thinks

00:29:06.010 --> 00:29:08.920 align:middle line:90%
they're going to go to the--

00:29:08.920 --> 00:29:09.940 align:middle line:90%
Sorry.

00:29:09.940 --> 00:29:13.150 align:middle line:90%
This is d, not delta.

00:29:13.150 --> 00:29:15.650 align:middle line:84%
The future self thinks they're
going to go to the gym,

00:29:15.650 --> 00:29:17.650 align:middle line:84%
but they're not actually
going to go to the gym.

00:29:17.650 --> 00:29:20.500 align:middle line:84%
Because you think that they're
going to be rational and trade

00:29:20.500 --> 00:29:24.820 align:middle line:84%
off benefit versus disutility,
but they're actually

00:29:24.820 --> 00:29:28.630 align:middle line:84%
going to trade off disutility
versus beta times benefit.

00:29:28.630 --> 00:29:31.210 align:middle line:90%
So this is-- oops.

00:29:31.210 --> 00:29:35.830 align:middle line:84%
So beta times b is less
than the disutility.

00:29:35.830 --> 00:29:36.637 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:29:36.637 --> 00:29:37.970 align:middle line:90%
Well, so here's what you can do.

00:29:37.970 --> 00:29:41.255 align:middle line:84%
You can tell that
consumer, OK, look.

00:29:41.255 --> 00:29:42.880 align:middle line:84%
What we're going to
do is go to the gym

00:29:42.880 --> 00:29:45.310 align:middle line:84%
and instead of charging
you cost every time you go,

00:29:45.310 --> 00:29:49.780 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to give you $10 less
than cost every time you go.

00:29:49.780 --> 00:29:52.000 align:middle line:90%
And so you're going to save--

00:29:52.000 --> 00:29:54.940 align:middle line:84%
you're going to earn $300
in these bonus payments

00:29:54.940 --> 00:29:56.890 align:middle line:84%
for going to the gym
every day, and so

00:29:56.890 --> 00:29:59.560 align:middle line:84%
for that, for the
low, low price of $299

00:29:59.560 --> 00:30:01.773 align:middle line:84%
a month, I'm going to give
you that $10 every day.

00:30:01.773 --> 00:30:03.190 align:middle line:84%
And the naive
hyperbolic consumers

00:30:03.190 --> 00:30:05.360 align:middle line:84%
are like, wow, that's
an awesome deal.

00:30:05.360 --> 00:30:06.880 align:middle line:84%
I have a negative
gym membership,

00:30:06.880 --> 00:30:10.670 align:middle line:84%
so they only pay 299 up front
and then I get $300 back.

00:30:10.670 --> 00:30:15.617 align:middle line:84%
But then obviously, you know,
you're never going to actually--

00:30:15.617 --> 00:30:17.700 align:middle line:84%
if the negative 10 is not
enough to get you to go,

00:30:17.700 --> 00:30:19.050 align:middle line:84%
you're never actually
going to get to go.

00:30:19.050 --> 00:30:20.633 align:middle line:84%
You've paid 299 up
front for something

00:30:20.633 --> 00:30:21.980 align:middle line:90%
that was worth zero to you.

00:30:21.980 --> 00:30:27.920 align:middle line:84%
And because you're going to
overestimate your gym usage,

00:30:27.920 --> 00:30:29.270 align:middle line:90%
you then end up--

00:30:29.270 --> 00:30:31.770 align:middle line:84%
you want to charge them
with that kind of scheme.

00:30:31.770 --> 00:30:35.030 align:middle line:90%


00:30:35.030 --> 00:30:36.380 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:30:36.380 --> 00:30:39.770 align:middle line:84%
Third observation here is
in this sophisticated model,

00:30:39.770 --> 00:30:42.490 align:middle line:90%
the p is socially efficient.

00:30:42.490 --> 00:30:44.070 align:middle line:90%
There isn't actually distortion.

00:30:44.070 --> 00:30:46.430 align:middle line:84%
You're charging
them less than cost,

00:30:46.430 --> 00:30:48.173 align:middle line:84%
but the price that
you charge them,

00:30:48.173 --> 00:30:50.090 align:middle line:84%
even though p is less--
we normally think of p

00:30:50.090 --> 00:30:53.003 align:middle line:84%
equals c as what gives us
full social efficiency.

00:30:53.003 --> 00:30:54.920 align:middle line:84%
Here it's actually p
less than c that gives us

00:30:54.920 --> 00:30:58.400 align:middle line:90%
full social efficiency.

00:30:58.400 --> 00:31:00.240 align:middle line:90%
Do I have that on here?

00:31:00.240 --> 00:31:00.740 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:31:00.740 --> 00:31:01.750 align:middle line:90%
Why is that?

00:31:01.750 --> 00:31:05.090 align:middle line:90%


00:31:05.090 --> 00:31:07.490 align:middle line:84%
You know, in some
ways this is like back

00:31:07.490 --> 00:31:09.425 align:middle line:90%
to lecture one of this class.

00:31:09.425 --> 00:31:11.300 align:middle line:84%
If you remember in
lecture one of this class,

00:31:11.300 --> 00:31:15.050 align:middle line:84%
I had if you have a firm
choosing product quality,

00:31:15.050 --> 00:31:17.995 align:middle line:84%
they choose the optimal quality
for the marginal consumer.

00:31:17.995 --> 00:31:21.108 align:middle line:90%


00:31:21.108 --> 00:31:23.190 align:middle line:84%
In the Della
Vigna-Malmendier paper,

00:31:23.190 --> 00:31:25.320 align:middle line:84%
all the consumers are
ex-ante identical,

00:31:25.320 --> 00:31:28.090 align:middle line:84%
so the marginal consumer
is the average consumer.

00:31:28.090 --> 00:31:30.240 align:middle line:84%
Therefore, what you do
is you always choose

00:31:30.240 --> 00:31:32.370 align:middle line:84%
the optimal product
quality and just extract

00:31:32.370 --> 00:31:34.805 align:middle line:90%
all of that with a fixed fee.

00:31:34.805 --> 00:31:36.220 align:middle line:90%
That's what this model is here.

00:31:36.220 --> 00:31:39.765 align:middle line:84%
It's like p is just a
dimension of quality.

00:31:39.765 --> 00:31:43.740 align:middle line:84%
A lower p gym contract, a
lower per visit contract,

00:31:43.740 --> 00:31:48.510 align:middle line:84%
is a contract that
has a higher quality,

00:31:48.510 --> 00:31:52.140 align:middle line:84%
and therefore, you just
choose the optimal quality

00:31:52.140 --> 00:31:54.770 align:middle line:84%
for the consumers and then
you extract all the fixed fee.

00:31:54.770 --> 00:31:56.880 align:middle line:84%
It's fully efficient
from the point

00:31:56.880 --> 00:31:59.280 align:middle line:84%
of view of the sophisticated
hyperbolic consumer who

00:31:59.280 --> 00:32:01.770 align:middle line:90%
is the one buying it.

00:32:01.770 --> 00:32:09.750 align:middle line:84%
And more generally, that
theorem from the first class

00:32:09.750 --> 00:32:14.825 align:middle line:84%
was that it's efficient in
terms of maximizing the sum of--

00:32:14.825 --> 00:32:16.200 align:middle line:84%
it's socially
efficient, in terms

00:32:16.200 --> 00:32:22.135 align:middle line:84%
of maximizing the sum
of profits of the firm

00:32:22.135 --> 00:32:23.525 align:middle line:90%
and the utility of the consumer.

00:32:23.525 --> 00:32:26.150 align:middle line:84%
But it wasn't really the utility
of the consumer that mattered.

00:32:26.150 --> 00:32:28.280 align:middle line:84%
It was the willingness
to pay of the consumer.

00:32:28.280 --> 00:32:30.700 align:middle line:84%
And so if you make the
willingness to pay different

00:32:30.700 --> 00:32:34.030 align:middle line:84%
from the utility,
then a monopolist

00:32:34.030 --> 00:32:35.650 align:middle line:84%
serving a homogeneous
population is

00:32:35.650 --> 00:32:39.850 align:middle line:84%
going to choose the quality that
maximizes the sum of profits

00:32:39.850 --> 00:32:41.042 align:middle line:90%
and willingness to pay.

00:32:41.042 --> 00:32:43.000 align:middle line:84%
And so it's going to be
efficient in that sense

00:32:43.000 --> 00:32:45.620 align:middle line:84%
of willingness to
pay as utility.

00:32:45.620 --> 00:32:48.040 align:middle line:84%
And so that's what we have in
the sophisticated hyperbolic

00:32:48.040 --> 00:32:48.590 align:middle line:90%
consumer.

00:32:48.590 --> 00:32:53.950 align:middle line:84%
You're always going to maximize
the utility that you get.

00:32:53.950 --> 00:32:56.830 align:middle line:90%
And this is going to--

00:32:56.830 --> 00:32:58.360 align:middle line:84%
in some ways, a
general lesson here

00:32:58.360 --> 00:33:01.270 align:middle line:84%
is that it's not
like competition

00:33:01.270 --> 00:33:05.440 align:middle line:84%
would force you to eliminate
these distorting consumers.

00:33:05.440 --> 00:33:08.290 align:middle line:84%
Competition forces you to
exploit behavioral consumers

00:33:08.290 --> 00:33:11.080 align:middle line:84%
because competition forces
you to design a product

00:33:11.080 --> 00:33:14.050 align:middle line:84%
quality or a contract
that maximizes willingness

00:33:14.050 --> 00:33:16.730 align:middle line:84%
to pay and then give all the
rents back to the consumers.

00:33:16.730 --> 00:33:19.210 align:middle line:84%
So whether it's monopoly
or perfect competition,

00:33:19.210 --> 00:33:22.240 align:middle line:84%
firms will always exploit
behavioral consumers

00:33:22.240 --> 00:33:24.010 align:middle line:84%
because they're
just always trying

00:33:24.010 --> 00:33:25.745 align:middle line:90%
to maximize willingness to pay.

00:33:25.745 --> 00:33:28.120 align:middle line:84%
And it doesn't matter that
willingness to pay is utility.

00:33:28.120 --> 00:33:30.190 align:middle line:84%
You're just trying to
maximize willingness to pay.

00:33:30.190 --> 00:33:32.190 align:middle line:84%
If willingness to pay and
utility are different,

00:33:32.190 --> 00:33:34.980 align:middle line:84%
you maximize willingness to
pay, which is exploiting people.

00:33:34.980 --> 00:33:39.880 align:middle line:90%


00:33:39.880 --> 00:33:44.950 align:middle line:84%
So they have one of the first
behavioral empirical papers

00:33:44.950 --> 00:33:51.670 align:middle line:84%
trying to analyze this idea
in a data set on health club

00:33:51.670 --> 00:33:55.540 align:middle line:84%
memberships in the
Boston area, and the idea

00:33:55.540 --> 00:33:58.720 align:middle line:90%
is it's basically a reduced--

00:33:58.720 --> 00:34:00.390 align:middle line:84%
one of these reduced
form papers where

00:34:00.390 --> 00:34:01.990 align:middle line:84%
we try to say, what
are predictions

00:34:01.990 --> 00:34:03.490 align:middle line:90%
of the rational model?

00:34:03.490 --> 00:34:07.180 align:middle line:84%
What would be not
necessarily predictions

00:34:07.180 --> 00:34:09.280 align:middle line:84%
of the hyperbolic
model, but directions

00:34:09.280 --> 00:34:12.190 align:middle line:84%
in which we think pricing
of hyperbolic consumers

00:34:12.190 --> 00:34:14.440 align:middle line:84%
would differ from pricing
with rational consumers?

00:34:14.440 --> 00:34:17.020 align:middle line:84%
And what they're trying to do
is reject the rational model

00:34:17.020 --> 00:34:19.570 align:middle line:84%
of health club
consumers and show

00:34:19.570 --> 00:34:21.550 align:middle line:84%
that some kind of
hyperbolic-- it's

00:34:21.550 --> 00:34:24.250 align:middle line:84%
departing from that
hyperbolic direction.

00:34:24.250 --> 00:34:29.620 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, the data set has
7,000 members at three Boston

00:34:29.620 --> 00:34:31.067 align:middle line:90%
area health clubs.

00:34:31.067 --> 00:34:32.650 align:middle line:84%
A lot of what they
have is just a data

00:34:32.650 --> 00:34:34.310 align:middle line:84%
set on the usage of
these health clubs,

00:34:34.310 --> 00:34:35.679 align:middle line:84%
and then they do also
have a survey data

00:34:35.679 --> 00:34:37.387 align:middle line:84%
where they stood
outside the health clubs

00:34:37.387 --> 00:34:40.370 align:middle line:84%
and just asked people survey
questions about how much they

00:34:40.370 --> 00:34:41.989 align:middle line:90%
use the gym and stuff.

00:34:41.989 --> 00:34:45.739 align:middle line:84%
So first, inconsistent with
the fully rational model.

00:34:45.739 --> 00:34:48.600 align:middle line:84%
Customers who sign up for
the gym just pay too much.

00:34:48.600 --> 00:34:51.800 align:middle line:90%


00:34:51.800 --> 00:34:55.909 align:middle line:84%
One of their plans their health
club offered was a $70 a month

00:34:55.909 --> 00:34:58.190 align:middle line:90%
unlimited gym pass.

00:34:58.190 --> 00:34:59.160 align:middle line:90%
You just pay $70.

00:34:59.160 --> 00:35:01.250 align:middle line:84%
You can go to the gym as
many times as you like.

00:35:01.250 --> 00:35:04.490 align:middle line:84%
Those who sign up for
the $70 a month plan

00:35:04.490 --> 00:35:07.230 align:middle line:84%
averaged visiting
4.3 times a month,

00:35:07.230 --> 00:35:10.160 align:middle line:84%
and so they were paying,
like, $17 every time

00:35:10.160 --> 00:35:11.113 align:middle line:90%
they went to the gym.

00:35:11.113 --> 00:35:13.280 align:middle line:84%
The health club also had a
plan where you could just

00:35:13.280 --> 00:35:18.650 align:middle line:84%
get a coupon book, which was 10
coupons for $100 where you just

00:35:18.650 --> 00:35:21.080 align:middle line:90%
pay $10 on a per visit basis.

00:35:21.080 --> 00:35:25.650 align:middle line:84%
Clearly it's not
fully rational model.

00:35:25.650 --> 00:35:27.578 align:middle line:84%
You should not be
paying $70 a month

00:35:27.578 --> 00:35:29.370 align:middle line:84%
for a gym you're going
to go to four times.

00:35:29.370 --> 00:35:32.420 align:middle line:84%
You should just buy the book
that gives you the 10 visits

00:35:32.420 --> 00:35:33.693 align:middle line:90%
and just use those coupons.

00:35:33.693 --> 00:35:36.470 align:middle line:90%


00:35:36.470 --> 00:35:39.440 align:middle line:84%
So either the consumers are
misestimating how often they're

00:35:39.440 --> 00:35:43.790 align:middle line:84%
going or the consumers
value the commitment

00:35:43.790 --> 00:35:46.085 align:middle line:84%
and are buying the gym
membership because if they

00:35:46.085 --> 00:35:48.710 align:middle line:84%
didn't buy the gym membership,
they would go zero times instead

00:35:48.710 --> 00:35:51.590 align:middle line:84%
of four times if they were
actually going to pay $10

00:35:51.590 --> 00:35:52.740 align:middle line:90%
every time they went.

00:35:52.740 --> 00:35:54.920 align:middle line:84%
So we don't know which
of those, but both

00:35:54.920 --> 00:35:56.930 align:middle line:84%
of those naive and
sophisticated hyperbolic

00:35:56.930 --> 00:35:59.480 align:middle line:84%
models predict that consumers
should be in this situation

00:35:59.480 --> 00:36:04.460 align:middle line:84%
where they're paying too much
ex-post for the gym visits.

00:36:04.460 --> 00:36:10.730 align:middle line:84%
Second thing of showing that
consumers are not fully rational

00:36:10.730 --> 00:36:15.530 align:middle line:84%
is this company had
two types of plans.

00:36:15.530 --> 00:36:19.280 align:middle line:84%
Besides the daily, the per visit
pass, they had a monthly pass

00:36:19.280 --> 00:36:22.430 align:middle line:90%
and they had an annual pass.

00:36:22.430 --> 00:36:25.160 align:middle line:84%
The monthly pass might
have been like, you know--

00:36:25.160 --> 00:36:26.300 align:middle line:90%
I forget the numbers.

00:36:26.300 --> 00:36:27.713 align:middle line:90%
$70 a month.

00:36:27.713 --> 00:36:29.630 align:middle line:84%
But then you also may
have had a choice of you

00:36:29.630 --> 00:36:34.850 align:middle line:84%
could also pay upfront $700
and get a full year membership,

00:36:34.850 --> 00:36:39.290 align:middle line:84%
paying less per month if you're
gung ho enough on the gym

00:36:39.290 --> 00:36:42.200 align:middle line:90%
that you would pay $700 fee.

00:36:42.200 --> 00:36:47.540 align:middle line:84%
And they note that people
who pay the $700 fee up front

00:36:47.540 --> 00:36:50.820 align:middle line:84%
do go to the gym more than the
people who pay the $70 a month.

00:36:50.820 --> 00:36:53.240 align:middle line:84%
So it's as if the people
who buy the annual pass

00:36:53.240 --> 00:36:55.370 align:middle line:84%
know that they are
people who go to the gym

00:36:55.370 --> 00:36:57.470 align:middle line:84%
and they're willing
to pay the $700

00:36:57.470 --> 00:37:00.590 align:middle line:84%
check up front to get
the annual membership.

00:37:00.590 --> 00:37:02.630 align:middle line:84%
But then one of the
interesting observations

00:37:02.630 --> 00:37:04.850 align:middle line:84%
is that the monthly
plan, they charge

00:37:04.850 --> 00:37:07.100 align:middle line:84%
to your credit card every
month until you do something

00:37:07.100 --> 00:37:08.330 align:middle line:90%
to get them to stop.

00:37:08.330 --> 00:37:10.850 align:middle line:84%
The $700 plan, you
actually have to-- it

00:37:10.850 --> 00:37:13.700 align:middle line:84%
didn't charge $700 to your
credit card a year from now.

00:37:13.700 --> 00:37:15.950 align:middle line:84%
You had to actually
go in and sign up

00:37:15.950 --> 00:37:17.750 align:middle line:90%
for another annual membership.

00:37:17.750 --> 00:37:20.990 align:middle line:84%
And what they find is if
you look 13 months later,

00:37:20.990 --> 00:37:23.210 align:middle line:84%
people who signed
up for the $70 plan

00:37:23.210 --> 00:37:25.580 align:middle line:84%
are more likely to be in
the gym than the people who

00:37:25.580 --> 00:37:27.980 align:middle line:90%
signed up for the $700 plan.

00:37:27.980 --> 00:37:30.047 align:middle line:84%
And it's because
these people just

00:37:30.047 --> 00:37:31.880 align:middle line:84%
got auto renewed if
they didn't do anything,

00:37:31.880 --> 00:37:35.060 align:middle line:84%
whereas these people had to
go in and actually decide,

00:37:35.060 --> 00:37:38.335 align:middle line:84%
yes, affirmatively, I do want to
still be signed up for the gym.

00:37:38.335 --> 00:37:40.710 align:middle line:84%
And so it's sometimes telling
that sometimes these people

00:37:40.710 --> 00:37:42.010 align:middle line:90%
aren't paying attention.

00:37:42.010 --> 00:37:45.420 align:middle line:84%
Another statistic about this was
that on average, their consumers

00:37:45.420 --> 00:37:50.490 align:middle line:84%
spend $187 after the very last
time they ever went to the gym.

00:37:50.490 --> 00:37:52.470 align:middle line:84%
So if you look at
people who quit the gym,

00:37:52.470 --> 00:37:57.390 align:middle line:84%
they've paid $187 since the very
last time that they visited.

00:37:57.390 --> 00:37:59.826 align:middle line:84%
This is saying that people
aren't paying attention--

00:37:59.826 --> 00:38:02.332 align:middle line:90%


00:38:02.332 --> 00:38:04.040 align:middle line:84%
aren't fully paying
attention and the gym

00:38:04.040 --> 00:38:06.770 align:middle line:84%
is designing a payment
scheme with the auto renewal

00:38:06.770 --> 00:38:09.600 align:middle line:84%
on your credit card
to exploit that.

00:38:09.600 --> 00:38:10.860 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:38:10.860 --> 00:38:14.460 align:middle line:84%
Other observations that suggest
the partially naive model

00:38:14.460 --> 00:38:19.410 align:middle line:84%
may be better than the fully
rational explanation for this.

00:38:19.410 --> 00:38:24.090 align:middle line:84%
You ask people in surveys, how
often will you go to the gym?

00:38:24.090 --> 00:38:26.040 align:middle line:84%
And people say
that they're going

00:38:26.040 --> 00:38:29.080 align:middle line:84%
to go to the gym more than twice
as much as they actually go.

00:38:29.080 --> 00:38:31.287 align:middle line:84%
So you ask the
people who have-- you

00:38:31.287 --> 00:38:33.870 align:middle line:84%
ask people who have signed up
for the plans, how many times do

00:38:33.870 --> 00:38:35.078 align:middle line:90%
you think you're going to go?

00:38:35.078 --> 00:38:37.470 align:middle line:90%
And they get that wrong.

00:38:37.470 --> 00:38:41.970 align:middle line:84%
And then also,
there's a correlation

00:38:41.970 --> 00:38:44.700 align:middle line:84%
between lower usage of the
gym and taking a longer

00:38:44.700 --> 00:38:47.080 align:middle line:84%
time to cancel after you
stop going to the gym.

00:38:47.080 --> 00:38:48.957 align:middle line:84%
And so this is a
sense that there

00:38:48.957 --> 00:38:50.790 align:middle line:84%
seem to be some people
who are more rational

00:38:50.790 --> 00:38:52.360 align:middle line:84%
and some people who
are less rational,

00:38:52.360 --> 00:38:53.735 align:middle line:84%
and then the less
rational people

00:38:53.735 --> 00:38:56.340 align:middle line:84%
do multiple things that
seem irrational together,

00:38:56.340 --> 00:38:59.850 align:middle line:84%
like the not canceling
and the not going so much.

00:38:59.850 --> 00:39:06.008 align:middle line:84%
Again, they don't estimate
a specific behavioral model

00:39:06.008 --> 00:39:08.300 align:middle line:84%
and show that specific
behavioral model can rationalize

00:39:08.300 --> 00:39:10.800 align:middle line:84%
all the things, but it's saying
that the rational model just

00:39:10.800 --> 00:39:12.800 align:middle line:90%
doesn't work.

00:39:12.800 --> 00:39:13.300 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:39:13.300 --> 00:39:16.060 align:middle line:90%


00:39:16.060 --> 00:39:16.560 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:39:16.560 --> 00:39:18.750 align:middle line:84%
So I thought next
thing I would do

00:39:18.750 --> 00:39:23.700 align:middle line:84%
is cover in more detail
one theoretical paper

00:39:23.700 --> 00:39:27.340 align:middle line:84%
to just give you more of a
sense of how these papers work.

00:39:27.340 --> 00:39:29.460 align:middle line:84%
So this is a paper by
Heidhues and Koszegi

00:39:29.460 --> 00:39:33.150 align:middle line:90%
about credit markets.

00:39:33.150 --> 00:39:38.640 align:middle line:84%
And I think there's a
feeling that many people have

00:39:38.640 --> 00:39:39.580 align:middle line:90%
too much debt.

00:39:39.580 --> 00:39:41.497 align:middle line:84%
In the last financial
crisis, there was a view

00:39:41.497 --> 00:39:42.880 align:middle line:90%
that people have too much debt.

00:39:42.880 --> 00:39:46.170 align:middle line:84%
There's also an observation that
many firms offer credit card

00:39:46.170 --> 00:39:49.440 align:middle line:84%
deals that look like things like
sign up for our credit card,

00:39:49.440 --> 00:39:53.280 align:middle line:84%
pay 0.9 interest for
the first six months

00:39:53.280 --> 00:39:57.010 align:middle line:84%
to pay off your other debts,
and then it goes up to 19.8%

00:39:57.010 --> 00:39:59.260 align:middle line:84%
And there's this use
of these teaser rates,

00:39:59.260 --> 00:40:01.718 align:middle line:84%
and then people think that,
are those teaser rates

00:40:01.718 --> 00:40:03.510 align:middle line:84%
designed to get people
to take on more debt

00:40:03.510 --> 00:40:06.720 align:middle line:84%
than they should take on or more
debt than they want to take on?

00:40:06.720 --> 00:40:10.530 align:middle line:84%
And so that's the idea of
the Heidhues-Koszegi paper is

00:40:10.530 --> 00:40:17.450 align:middle line:84%
to think about, would
quasi-hyperbolic consumers--

00:40:17.450 --> 00:40:20.540 align:middle line:84%
would the market exploit
quasi-hyperbolic consumers

00:40:20.540 --> 00:40:23.990 align:middle line:84%
in a way that creates them
taking on too much debt

00:40:23.990 --> 00:40:26.360 align:middle line:84%
and would it
explain why it is we

00:40:26.360 --> 00:40:32.110 align:middle line:84%
see that these contracts that
have initial upfront teaser

00:40:32.110 --> 00:40:36.510 align:middle line:84%
rates followed by
higher rates later on?

00:40:36.510 --> 00:40:38.140 align:middle line:90%
So in their model--

00:40:38.140 --> 00:40:42.310 align:middle line:84%
and this becomes-- it's
a nice paper in that it's

00:40:42.310 --> 00:40:45.640 align:middle line:84%
a paper that shows there's this
very, very sharp discontinuity

00:40:45.640 --> 00:40:51.460 align:middle line:84%
where, with fully sophisticated
quasi-hyperbolic consumers,

00:40:51.460 --> 00:40:54.670 align:middle line:84%
one thing happens and then you
make even an epsilon difference

00:40:54.670 --> 00:40:58.820 align:middle line:84%
between their self-recognition
and full self-recognition,

00:40:58.820 --> 00:41:00.730 align:middle line:84%
you get a big discrete
jump in behavior

00:41:00.730 --> 00:41:05.230 align:middle line:84%
with an epsilon change in
the behavioral assumption.

00:41:05.230 --> 00:41:06.940 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, we have
this agent, they

00:41:06.940 --> 00:41:09.448 align:middle line:84%
have the beta, beta
hat one preferences.

00:41:09.448 --> 00:41:10.990 align:middle line:84%
So just we're
eliminating discounting

00:41:10.990 --> 00:41:12.820 align:middle line:90%
because t equals three.

00:41:12.820 --> 00:41:15.340 align:middle line:84%
So the consumer is going
to borrow some money at t

00:41:15.340 --> 00:41:17.440 align:middle line:90%
equals zero.

00:41:17.440 --> 00:41:21.220 align:middle line:84%
Think of this as
taking out a car loan.

00:41:21.220 --> 00:41:23.590 align:middle line:84%
I'm borrowing some
money c to buy a car,

00:41:23.590 --> 00:41:25.870 align:middle line:84%
and then I buy a
durable good and I

00:41:25.870 --> 00:41:31.450 align:middle line:84%
get utility from the durable
good at t equals one.

00:41:31.450 --> 00:41:34.060 align:middle line:84%
So there's some
sense there's a time

00:41:34.060 --> 00:41:37.390 align:middle line:84%
zero where I pay the
money and then a time one

00:41:37.390 --> 00:41:40.008 align:middle line:84%
where I enjoy the durable
good, and then time two,

00:41:40.008 --> 00:41:41.800 align:middle line:84%
I could still be enjoying
the durable good.

00:41:41.800 --> 00:41:43.870 align:middle line:84%
They do that the durable
good is already worn out

00:41:43.870 --> 00:41:45.537 align:middle line:84%
and I still just have
my debt for my car

00:41:45.537 --> 00:41:47.080 align:middle line:90%
after I've already sold my car.

00:41:47.080 --> 00:41:49.640 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, there's
I borrowed money.

00:41:49.640 --> 00:41:52.180 align:middle line:84%
I get utility of t equals
one from my durable good.

00:41:52.180 --> 00:41:55.540 align:middle line:84%
At t equals one I'm making my
car payments, and then at t

00:41:55.540 --> 00:41:57.930 align:middle line:84%
equals two, I'm still making
an additional car payment.

00:41:57.930 --> 00:42:01.160 align:middle line:90%


00:42:01.160 --> 00:42:03.080 align:middle line:84%
So they have this
conflict of interest

00:42:03.080 --> 00:42:07.760 align:middle line:84%
between how the different selves
will view the car payments

00:42:07.760 --> 00:42:09.540 align:middle line:90%
versus the enjoying the car.

00:42:09.540 --> 00:42:11.960 align:middle line:84%
So from the point of view
of the time zero self, which

00:42:11.960 --> 00:42:15.075 align:middle line:84%
is before he's going
to enjoy the car

00:42:15.075 --> 00:42:16.950 align:middle line:84%
and before he's going
to make the repayments,

00:42:16.950 --> 00:42:22.910 align:middle line:84%
time zero self's preferences
are c minus k of q minus k of r.

00:42:22.910 --> 00:42:29.870 align:middle line:84%
So there's this convex
function about your repayment.

00:42:29.870 --> 00:42:33.050 align:middle line:84%
You have this convex
disutility of a repayment

00:42:33.050 --> 00:42:35.630 align:middle line:84%
because the more you have
to repay in your monthly car

00:42:35.630 --> 00:42:37.880 align:middle line:84%
payment, the more you're
cutting back on other things,

00:42:37.880 --> 00:42:41.000 align:middle line:84%
and the more things you cut
back on increasing disutility

00:42:41.000 --> 00:42:42.470 align:middle line:84%
for cutting back
on food as you're

00:42:42.470 --> 00:42:44.525 align:middle line:90%
cutting back more and more.

00:42:44.525 --> 00:42:46.580 align:middle line:84%
And so the time
zero self utility

00:42:46.580 --> 00:42:49.430 align:middle line:84%
is c is the enjoyment
of the car minus--

00:42:49.430 --> 00:42:51.383 align:middle line:84%
all these things would
be multiplied by beta

00:42:51.383 --> 00:42:52.800 align:middle line:84%
because they're
all in the future,

00:42:52.800 --> 00:42:55.350 align:middle line:84%
but we ignore that fact because
they're all in the future.

00:42:55.350 --> 00:42:57.850 align:middle line:84%
So from the time zero self,
these things are all comparable,

00:42:57.850 --> 00:43:00.975 align:middle line:84%
and it's c minus k
of q minus k of r.

00:43:00.975 --> 00:43:03.690 align:middle line:90%


00:43:03.690 --> 00:43:06.550 align:middle line:84%
From the time one's
perspective self,

00:43:06.550 --> 00:43:11.470 align:middle line:84%
time one cares about the car
and the repayment made at t

00:43:11.470 --> 00:43:12.560 align:middle line:90%
equals one.

00:43:12.560 --> 00:43:15.693 align:middle line:84%
By the time one self
discounts the pain

00:43:15.693 --> 00:43:17.110 align:middle line:84%
he's going to
suffer repaying at t

00:43:17.110 --> 00:43:19.540 align:middle line:84%
equals two because
that's a future thing.

00:43:19.540 --> 00:43:22.540 align:middle line:84%
And then by the time it gets to
the time two self, the time two

00:43:22.540 --> 00:43:25.240 align:middle line:84%
self dislikes the
repayment that the time two

00:43:25.240 --> 00:43:26.535 align:middle line:90%
self is being forced to make.

00:43:26.535 --> 00:43:31.630 align:middle line:90%


00:43:31.630 --> 00:43:33.610 align:middle line:84%
We're going to assume
that the credit market is

00:43:33.610 --> 00:43:34.970 align:middle line:90%
perfectly competitive.

00:43:34.970 --> 00:43:36.940 align:middle line:90%
It has zero interest rates.

00:43:36.940 --> 00:43:39.970 align:middle line:84%
And firms can offer
these option contracts

00:43:39.970 --> 00:43:42.940 align:middle line:84%
where you pay, you
get some amount of c

00:43:42.940 --> 00:43:45.370 align:middle line:84%
is given to you
up front, and then

00:43:45.370 --> 00:43:49.180 align:middle line:84%
you have these repayment
plans you can choose among,

00:43:49.180 --> 00:43:52.787 align:middle line:84%
q sub s, r sub s,
and at t equals one

00:43:52.787 --> 00:43:54.620 align:middle line:84%
you choose how you're
how you want to repay.

00:43:54.620 --> 00:43:56.620 align:middle line:84%
Do you want to make the
immediate repayment at t

00:43:56.620 --> 00:43:57.230 align:middle line:90%
equals one?

00:43:57.230 --> 00:43:59.890 align:middle line:84%
Do you want to delay your
payment and spread it out over t

00:43:59.890 --> 00:44:01.460 align:middle line:90%
equals one and t equals two?

00:44:01.460 --> 00:44:04.790 align:middle line:84%
Or maybe they have many, many
different possible plans.

00:44:04.790 --> 00:44:05.290 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:44:05.290 --> 00:44:07.930 align:middle line:90%


00:44:07.930 --> 00:44:09.920 align:middle line:84%
Suppose we have a
rational consumer.

00:44:09.920 --> 00:44:11.900 align:middle line:84%
What does a rational
consumer do?

00:44:11.900 --> 00:44:15.340 align:middle line:84%
Well, the rational consumer,
you're just maximizing this,

00:44:15.340 --> 00:44:17.860 align:middle line:84%
and there's going to be
a break even constraint

00:44:17.860 --> 00:44:19.960 align:middle line:84%
in the competitive market
that is going to have

00:44:19.960 --> 00:44:22.930 align:middle line:90%
to be c equals q plus r.

00:44:22.930 --> 00:44:25.720 align:middle line:84%
The competitive
interest lenders are

00:44:25.720 --> 00:44:29.250 align:middle line:84%
going to always offer you the
full amount of the repayments

00:44:29.250 --> 00:44:31.410 align:middle line:84%
as your upfront
price of the loan.

00:44:31.410 --> 00:44:35.270 align:middle line:84%
So we're just trying
to maximize this.

00:44:35.270 --> 00:44:41.120 align:middle line:84%
So we're trying to
maximize c minus k of q

00:44:41.120 --> 00:44:47.590 align:middle line:84%
minus k of r subject to
the zero profit constraint.

00:44:47.590 --> 00:44:48.775 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:44:48.775 --> 00:44:52.660 align:middle line:90%
What's the solution to that?

00:44:52.660 --> 00:44:54.100 align:middle line:90%
Lagrangians are all ones.

00:44:54.100 --> 00:44:55.630 align:middle line:84%
You differentiate
with respect to q,

00:44:55.630 --> 00:44:58.570 align:middle line:84%
and the repayments
are going to have

00:44:58.570 --> 00:45:04.270 align:middle line:84%
to satisfy the marginal
disutility of repaying a dollar

00:45:04.270 --> 00:45:08.380 align:middle line:84%
when you have a payment
of q is equal to one

00:45:08.380 --> 00:45:13.830 align:middle line:84%
and the marginal value
of repaying a dollar

00:45:13.830 --> 00:45:15.970 align:middle line:84%
when you're paying
r is equal to one.

00:45:15.970 --> 00:45:21.750 align:middle line:84%
And so you repay evenly,
setting these repayment rates

00:45:21.750 --> 00:45:24.610 align:middle line:84%
so that the disutility of
paying that dollar is one,

00:45:24.610 --> 00:45:26.790 align:middle line:84%
and then you set c
equal to q plus r.

00:45:26.790 --> 00:45:29.460 align:middle line:90%


00:45:29.460 --> 00:45:32.910 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, the k prime at
q equals one pins down

00:45:32.910 --> 00:45:34.920 align:middle line:84%
how big the loan
should be, and then

00:45:34.920 --> 00:45:38.535 align:middle line:84%
you just repay evenly
over the two periods.

00:45:38.535 --> 00:45:41.580 align:middle line:84%
And the rational loan
contract doesn't have

00:45:41.580 --> 00:45:42.850 align:middle line:90%
to be some complicated thing.

00:45:42.850 --> 00:45:46.320 align:middle line:84%
The rational loan contract just
says, you know, here you go.

00:45:46.320 --> 00:45:47.320 align:middle line:90%
You get no choices.

00:45:47.320 --> 00:45:50.610 align:middle line:84%
You get c first best, and
then you make those payments

00:45:50.610 --> 00:45:55.380 align:middle line:90%
q first best, r first best.

00:45:55.380 --> 00:45:57.035 align:middle line:84%
There's exactly one
repayment option.

00:45:57.035 --> 00:45:58.160 align:middle line:90%
That's what you have to do.

00:45:58.160 --> 00:46:05.880 align:middle line:90%


00:46:05.880 --> 00:46:08.190 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:46:08.190 --> 00:46:13.680 align:middle line:84%
Imagine that the consumers
are sophisticated.

00:46:13.680 --> 00:46:15.880 align:middle line:84%
So you have
sophisticated hyperbolic.

00:46:15.880 --> 00:46:19.080 align:middle line:84%
So the consumers are hyperbolic,
but they're sophisticated so

00:46:19.080 --> 00:46:20.790 align:middle line:84%
that they have beta
delta preferences,

00:46:20.790 --> 00:46:24.080 align:middle line:84%
but they recognize their future
self as beta delta preference.

00:46:24.080 --> 00:46:29.910 align:middle line:84%
Then the time zero
consumer, though hyperbolic,

00:46:29.910 --> 00:46:32.230 align:middle line:84%
the time zero consumer here
is not getting the benefit.

00:46:32.230 --> 00:46:33.780 align:middle line:84%
It's the time one
and two consumers

00:46:33.780 --> 00:46:35.370 align:middle line:90%
who get the benefit of the car.

00:46:35.370 --> 00:46:40.200 align:middle line:84%
So the time zero consumer has
essentially rational preferences

00:46:40.200 --> 00:46:42.930 align:middle line:84%
over what he wants the
future selves to do.

00:46:42.930 --> 00:46:47.250 align:middle line:84%
So in a sophisticated
hyperbolic model,

00:46:47.250 --> 00:46:50.070 align:middle line:84%
a sophisticated
hyperbolic model,

00:46:50.070 --> 00:46:54.090 align:middle line:84%
the monopolist is still going
to offer the rational contract.

00:46:54.090 --> 00:46:56.070 align:middle line:84%
And so you're going to
commit your future self

00:46:56.070 --> 00:46:58.080 align:middle line:84%
to make those two even
payments, because that's

00:46:58.080 --> 00:47:00.840 align:middle line:84%
what is best for
the future selves

00:47:00.840 --> 00:47:03.540 align:middle line:84%
to do from the point of view
of the time zero purchaser.

00:47:03.540 --> 00:47:08.540 align:middle line:84%
So in some sense,
a lending market

00:47:08.540 --> 00:47:11.060 align:middle line:84%
sells commitment to the
time zero consumers who

00:47:11.060 --> 00:47:13.130 align:middle line:84%
want commitment and
lets them overcome

00:47:13.130 --> 00:47:15.830 align:middle line:90%
their hyperbolic preferences.

00:47:15.830 --> 00:47:20.210 align:middle line:84%
Again, they get
their first best.

00:47:20.210 --> 00:47:23.300 align:middle line:84%
We get the first best buy
just committed to the contract

00:47:23.300 --> 00:47:24.087 align:middle line:90%
is the first best.

00:47:24.087 --> 00:47:25.045 align:middle line:90%
That's the only option.

00:47:25.045 --> 00:47:28.310 align:middle line:90%


00:47:28.310 --> 00:47:30.800 align:middle line:84%
Then the interesting
theorem is what

00:47:30.800 --> 00:47:34.370 align:middle line:84%
happens when beta hat
is different from beta.

00:47:34.370 --> 00:47:37.010 align:middle line:84%
The result is that when beta
hat is greater than beta,

00:47:37.010 --> 00:47:40.830 align:middle line:84%
you think you're even epsilon
less hyperbolic than you are.

00:47:40.830 --> 00:47:43.310 align:middle line:84%
There's a discontinuity
in the contract.

00:47:43.310 --> 00:47:48.020 align:middle line:84%
And instead of offering just
this straightforward no option

00:47:48.020 --> 00:47:52.610 align:middle line:84%
contract, firms now
offer an option contract.

00:47:52.610 --> 00:47:56.000 align:middle line:84%
The contract looks like you
get some amount c up front,

00:47:56.000 --> 00:47:58.500 align:middle line:84%
and then you have two
repayment options.

00:47:58.500 --> 00:48:01.640 align:middle line:84%
One is to repay
everything at t equals one

00:48:01.640 --> 00:48:03.920 align:middle line:84%
and get a very good deal
repaying everything at t

00:48:03.920 --> 00:48:07.130 align:middle line:84%
equals one, and then
the other one is

00:48:07.130 --> 00:48:10.460 align:middle line:84%
to spread your repayments
out over t equals one

00:48:10.460 --> 00:48:14.030 align:middle line:84%
and t equals two, and make
an even larger repayment at t

00:48:14.030 --> 00:48:17.600 align:middle line:84%
equals two than you
make at t equals one.

00:48:17.600 --> 00:48:20.930 align:middle line:84%
So you can think of it
as like you have these--

00:48:20.930 --> 00:48:22.715 align:middle line:84%
you know, they give
you money up front.

00:48:22.715 --> 00:48:25.460 align:middle line:90%


00:48:25.460 --> 00:48:28.850 align:middle line:84%
Let's suppose they give you
$30,000 up front and then they

00:48:28.850 --> 00:48:31.740 align:middle line:84%
have these two options
for repayments.

00:48:31.740 --> 00:48:37.190 align:middle line:84%
One is to repay 25,000 at t
equals one and zero at t equals

00:48:37.190 --> 00:48:42.440 align:middle line:84%
two, and the other is to
repay 15,000 at t equals one

00:48:42.440 --> 00:48:50.800 align:middle line:84%
and 25,000 or 20,000
at t equals two.

00:48:50.800 --> 00:48:51.300 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:48:51.300 --> 00:48:54.570 align:middle line:84%
So the first best in my simple
example would be they loan you

00:48:54.570 --> 00:49:01.660 align:middle line:84%
30,000 and you repay 15,000 in
each of the two future periods.

00:49:01.660 --> 00:49:04.680 align:middle line:84%
That's what the first efficient
thing would be in this model.

00:49:04.680 --> 00:49:07.600 align:middle line:84%
But instead what they do
is they actually loan you--

00:49:07.600 --> 00:49:14.790 align:middle line:84%
actually, they do following,
they loan you 35,000 and they

00:49:14.790 --> 00:49:15.960 align:middle line:90%
give you two options.

00:49:15.960 --> 00:49:19.950 align:middle line:84%
Either repay 25,000 at t equals
one and nothing at t equals two,

00:49:19.950 --> 00:49:26.920 align:middle line:84%
or repay 15,000 at t equals
one and 25,000 at t equals two.

00:49:26.920 --> 00:49:31.720 align:middle line:84%
And so the contract has
two different distortions.

00:49:31.720 --> 00:49:36.490 align:middle line:84%
One distortion is that
the consumers end up

00:49:36.490 --> 00:49:40.870 align:middle line:84%
taking on more debt
than they want--

00:49:40.870 --> 00:49:42.550 align:middle line:84%
take on more debt
than they want,

00:49:42.550 --> 00:49:44.830 align:middle line:84%
more debt than they should
in a rational contract

00:49:44.830 --> 00:49:48.630 align:middle line:84%
and get a bigger loan and
buy a more expensive car.

00:49:48.630 --> 00:49:50.460 align:middle line:84%
And then the second
distortion is

00:49:50.460 --> 00:49:54.840 align:middle line:84%
that they end up choosing the
wrong repayment option, where

00:49:54.840 --> 00:49:56.492 align:middle line:84%
you give them this
option, they think

00:49:56.492 --> 00:49:57.700 align:middle line:90%
they're going to choose this.

00:49:57.700 --> 00:49:58.870 align:middle line:90%
Wow, that's a great deal.

00:49:58.870 --> 00:50:00.870 align:middle line:84%
If I just pay everything
back next period,

00:50:00.870 --> 00:50:03.640 align:middle line:84%
I'm getting an awesome
deal repaying my car loan.

00:50:03.640 --> 00:50:06.720 align:middle line:84%
But then in fact, once it gets
the next period and trying

00:50:06.720 --> 00:50:10.670 align:middle line:84%
to come up with 25,000 all
in one year is too painful,

00:50:10.670 --> 00:50:12.510 align:middle line:84%
you end up spreading
it out and saying, OK,

00:50:12.510 --> 00:50:18.228 align:middle line:84%
I'll pay 15,000 today
and then 25,000 tomorrow.

00:50:18.228 --> 00:50:21.780 align:middle line:84%
So we get these two different
distortions, the distortion

00:50:21.780 --> 00:50:25.860 align:middle line:84%
of loans that are too big
and loan payments that

00:50:25.860 --> 00:50:27.480 align:middle line:84%
are distorted with
a balloon payment

00:50:27.480 --> 00:50:30.510 align:middle line:84%
at the end that's larger than
the payment in the beginning,

00:50:30.510 --> 00:50:33.900 align:middle line:84%
whereas spreading the payments
evenly is what would minimize

00:50:33.900 --> 00:50:37.500 align:middle line:90%
the customer's disutility.

00:50:37.500 --> 00:50:38.000 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:50:38.000 --> 00:50:40.890 align:middle line:84%
And, you know, it's a model
with perfect competition.

00:50:40.890 --> 00:50:43.880 align:middle line:84%
So with perfect
competition, the lenders--

00:50:43.880 --> 00:50:47.240 align:middle line:84%
actually, so I should
have done this with 20.

00:50:47.240 --> 00:50:50.300 align:middle line:84%
In this model, the
lenders, zero profits.

00:50:50.300 --> 00:50:52.020 align:middle line:84%
You get zero profits
for the lenders,

00:50:52.020 --> 00:50:53.960 align:middle line:84%
so the amount that
they borrow is

00:50:53.960 --> 00:50:56.040 align:middle line:90%
exactly equal to the repayment.

00:50:56.040 --> 00:50:58.670 align:middle line:84%
So it's not like lenders make
any extra-- lenders don't

00:50:58.670 --> 00:50:59.460 align:middle line:90%
make any money.

00:50:59.460 --> 00:51:02.000 align:middle line:84%
It's just utility
is being destroyed

00:51:02.000 --> 00:51:06.470 align:middle line:84%
by having consumers get a larger
loan than is rational for them

00:51:06.470 --> 00:51:12.110 align:middle line:84%
to get and pay the
money back unevenly

00:51:12.110 --> 00:51:14.990 align:middle line:84%
in a way that makes them
suffer more disutility

00:51:14.990 --> 00:51:16.393 align:middle line:90%
from the unequal payments.

00:51:16.393 --> 00:51:23.220 align:middle line:90%


00:51:23.220 --> 00:51:27.245 align:middle line:84%
So questions on the finding of
what they're trying to show?

00:51:27.245 --> 00:51:30.550 align:middle line:90%


00:51:30.550 --> 00:51:31.050 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:51:31.050 --> 00:51:33.717 align:middle line:84%
So what I thought I would do is
I would actually just go through

00:51:33.717 --> 00:51:38.920 align:middle line:84%
the-- it's a standard type
of mechanism design argument.

00:51:38.920 --> 00:51:41.230 align:middle line:84%
It's pretty simple, so I
thought I would do that.

00:51:41.230 --> 00:51:44.040 align:middle line:84%
So first way to think about
analyzing a model like this

00:51:44.040 --> 00:51:49.950 align:middle line:84%
is we can restrict
ourselves to contracts

00:51:49.950 --> 00:51:53.460 align:middle line:84%
that have just two options,
because all that really matters

00:51:53.460 --> 00:51:55.620 align:middle line:84%
is what does the
consumer think they're

00:51:55.620 --> 00:51:58.680 align:middle line:84%
going to repay when
they sign the contract

00:51:58.680 --> 00:52:00.333 align:middle line:90%
and what do they actually repay.

00:52:00.333 --> 00:52:03.000 align:middle line:84%
So there's one option that needs
to be there, which is what they

00:52:03.000 --> 00:52:03.880 align:middle line:90%
think they're going to pay.

00:52:03.880 --> 00:52:05.980 align:middle line:84%
There's another one, what are
they actually going to pay?

00:52:05.980 --> 00:52:07.840 align:middle line:84%
You can put 10 different
options in there.

00:52:07.840 --> 00:52:09.755 align:middle line:90%
The other eight don't matter.

00:52:09.755 --> 00:52:12.090 align:middle line:84%
OK, so we can think of a
general class of contracts

00:52:12.090 --> 00:52:14.370 align:middle line:84%
as being how much
money does the lenders

00:52:14.370 --> 00:52:17.513 align:middle line:84%
give them up front, what's
the repayment option

00:52:17.513 --> 00:52:19.680 align:middle line:84%
that they think they're
going to take when they sign

00:52:19.680 --> 00:52:21.930 align:middle line:84%
the contract, and then
what's the repayment

00:52:21.930 --> 00:52:24.568 align:middle line:84%
option that they're
actually going to take.

00:52:24.568 --> 00:52:25.068 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:52:25.068 --> 00:52:27.990 align:middle line:90%


00:52:27.990 --> 00:52:30.690 align:middle line:84%
The lenders are competing to
make the most attractive offers,

00:52:30.690 --> 00:52:32.850 align:middle line:84%
which means it must
be that the offer that

00:52:32.850 --> 00:52:36.540 align:middle line:84%
gets made and accepted in
equilibrium solves this problem.

00:52:36.540 --> 00:52:39.480 align:middle line:84%
We're maximizing the
utility that consumers

00:52:39.480 --> 00:52:44.050 align:middle line:84%
think you're giving them
subject to three constraints.

00:52:44.050 --> 00:52:46.770 align:middle line:84%
The first is the individual
rationality constraint

00:52:46.770 --> 00:52:48.510 align:middle line:84%
of the lenders,
which is the lenders

00:52:48.510 --> 00:52:52.040 align:middle line:84%
have to earn zero
profits, so the amount

00:52:52.040 --> 00:52:53.790 align:middle line:84%
that they give the
consumer up front can't

00:52:53.790 --> 00:52:55.665 align:middle line:84%
be any bigger than the
repayment that they're

00:52:55.665 --> 00:52:59.950 align:middle line:84%
going to get in equilibrium,
which is q and r.

00:52:59.950 --> 00:53:02.860 align:middle line:84%
There's the IC constraint
for the time zero self,

00:53:02.860 --> 00:53:04.520 align:middle line:90%
the one making the purchase.

00:53:04.520 --> 00:53:07.210 align:middle line:84%
The time zero self has
to think that they're

00:53:07.210 --> 00:53:10.550 align:middle line:90%
going to repay q hat r hat.

00:53:10.550 --> 00:53:16.320 align:middle line:84%
And so it must be that
the disutility that you

00:53:16.320 --> 00:53:19.440 align:middle line:84%
get from paying q hat r
hat, which is k of q hat

00:53:19.440 --> 00:53:22.980 align:middle line:84%
plus beta hat k of r hat, that's
how you think your future self

00:53:22.980 --> 00:53:27.990 align:middle line:84%
is going to be hyperbolic,
that has to be less than k of q

00:53:27.990 --> 00:53:29.200 align:middle line:90%
plus beta hat k of r.

00:53:29.200 --> 00:53:33.300 align:middle line:84%
So you think, wow, this
one is such a bad--

00:53:33.300 --> 00:53:35.190 align:middle line:84%
IC zero says this
is such a bad deal

00:53:35.190 --> 00:53:39.060 align:middle line:84%
relative to this one that my
future self will pay this one.

00:53:39.060 --> 00:53:42.600 align:middle line:84%
And then it must also be that
when we get to t equals one,

00:53:42.600 --> 00:53:44.580 align:middle line:84%
from the t equals
one self, when the t

00:53:44.580 --> 00:53:46.740 align:middle line:84%
equals one self is
comparing these,

00:53:46.740 --> 00:53:49.240 align:middle line:84%
the t equals one self
says, wow, this is painful.

00:53:49.240 --> 00:53:51.030 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to choose
this one instead.

00:53:51.030 --> 00:53:54.730 align:middle line:84%
So those are the
two IC constraints.

00:53:54.730 --> 00:53:56.670 align:middle line:84%
The t equals zero
self has to say,

00:53:56.670 --> 00:53:59.820 align:middle line:84%
I think I'm going to take
the q hat r hat option,

00:53:59.820 --> 00:54:03.210 align:middle line:84%
and then the t equals
one self has to say,

00:54:03.210 --> 00:54:05.313 align:middle line:84%
that q hat r hat
option is painful.

00:54:05.313 --> 00:54:06.980 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to take the
qr option instead.

00:54:06.980 --> 00:54:09.590 align:middle line:90%


00:54:09.590 --> 00:54:10.310 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:54:10.310 --> 00:54:14.660 align:middle line:84%
So we have this
fairly simple problem

00:54:14.660 --> 00:54:21.530 align:middle line:84%
that initially has five
unknowns, c, q, r, q hat, r hat.

00:54:21.530 --> 00:54:24.560 align:middle line:84%
And as you often do in
mechanism design problems,

00:54:24.560 --> 00:54:28.910 align:middle line:84%
the way to solve these things is
to start by eliminating as many

00:54:28.910 --> 00:54:31.760 align:middle line:84%
of the constraints as you
can or turning the inequality

00:54:31.760 --> 00:54:33.440 align:middle line:84%
constraints into
equality constraints

00:54:33.440 --> 00:54:35.480 align:middle line:90%
and eliminating variables.

00:54:35.480 --> 00:54:37.520 align:middle line:84%
The first variable
I'm going to note

00:54:37.520 --> 00:54:41.130 align:middle line:84%
is the IR constraint is
always going to be binding.

00:54:41.130 --> 00:54:44.120 align:middle line:84%
You're always going to
set c equals q plus r.

00:54:44.120 --> 00:54:45.830 align:middle line:84%
You're going to set
c equals q plus r,

00:54:45.830 --> 00:54:48.680 align:middle line:84%
because giving the
consumer more money

00:54:48.680 --> 00:54:52.020 align:middle line:84%
up front makes the
consumer happier,

00:54:52.020 --> 00:54:54.840 align:middle line:84%
and this is the only
constraint where c appears.

00:54:54.840 --> 00:54:59.990 align:middle line:84%
So clearly we can really drop
that out and just say whatever q

00:54:59.990 --> 00:55:02.155 align:middle line:84%
and r we choose, c is
going to be q plus r.

00:55:02.155 --> 00:55:05.440 align:middle line:90%


00:55:05.440 --> 00:55:06.010 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:55:06.010 --> 00:55:11.350 align:middle line:84%
Second observation
is that this one also

00:55:11.350 --> 00:55:13.300 align:middle line:90%
has to hold with equality.

00:55:13.300 --> 00:55:16.840 align:middle line:84%
If this constraint
was non-binding,

00:55:16.840 --> 00:55:19.570 align:middle line:90%
I can make q and r bigger.

00:55:19.570 --> 00:55:22.180 align:middle line:84%
And if I make q and r bigger
this constraint, if this

00:55:22.180 --> 00:55:24.010 align:middle line:84%
was non-binding, I'd
make q and r bigger,

00:55:24.010 --> 00:55:25.880 align:middle line:90%
this would still be satisfied.

00:55:25.880 --> 00:55:28.142 align:middle line:84%
This would be satisfied,
this is satisfied,

00:55:28.142 --> 00:55:29.350 align:middle line:90%
this doesn't involve q and r.

00:55:29.350 --> 00:55:33.070 align:middle line:84%
So I'm always going to want to
make q and r as big as I can

00:55:33.070 --> 00:55:35.870 align:middle line:84%
until this constraint
gets satisfied.

00:55:35.870 --> 00:55:39.940 align:middle line:84%
So what limits these repayments
is that the consumer at t

00:55:39.940 --> 00:55:42.730 align:middle line:84%
equals one has to actually
be willing to make them.

00:55:42.730 --> 00:55:45.906 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, so this one
holds with equality.

00:55:45.906 --> 00:55:48.650 align:middle line:90%


00:55:48.650 --> 00:55:52.820 align:middle line:84%
So once you know that this
one has an equal sign in it,

00:55:52.820 --> 00:55:57.020 align:middle line:84%
think about what does this
constraint equivalent to when

00:55:57.020 --> 00:55:58.347 align:middle line:90%
this one holds with equality?

00:55:58.347 --> 00:55:59.930 align:middle line:84%
Well, if this one
holds with equality,

00:55:59.930 --> 00:56:03.080 align:middle line:84%
I can just subtract the two
left side-- the left side here

00:56:03.080 --> 00:56:05.760 align:middle line:84%
from this, subtract the
right side here from this,

00:56:05.760 --> 00:56:07.760 align:middle line:90%
and both things are still true.

00:56:07.760 --> 00:56:11.090 align:middle line:84%
And so the top inequality still
holds, and these things cancel,

00:56:11.090 --> 00:56:12.690 align:middle line:90%
and these things cancel.

00:56:12.690 --> 00:56:14.900 align:middle line:84%
And so what I find is
that it must be true

00:56:14.900 --> 00:56:20.900 align:middle line:84%
that beta hat minus beta k of r,
the right side, this minus this,

00:56:20.900 --> 00:56:26.750 align:middle line:84%
is bigger than beta hat,
minus beta times k of r hat.

00:56:26.750 --> 00:56:29.390 align:middle line:90%
So the IC constraint.

00:56:29.390 --> 00:56:31.550 align:middle line:84%
So once this holds
with equality,

00:56:31.550 --> 00:56:35.360 align:middle line:84%
this constraint is simply
equivalent to r hat

00:56:35.360 --> 00:56:36.325 align:middle line:90%
being less than r.

00:56:36.325 --> 00:56:38.740 align:middle line:90%


00:56:38.740 --> 00:56:39.240 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:56:39.240 --> 00:56:42.520 align:middle line:84%
So IC zero is just this
holds with equality,

00:56:42.520 --> 00:56:44.610 align:middle line:84%
this holds with
equality, this is

00:56:44.610 --> 00:56:46.850 align:middle line:90%
equivalent to r hat less than r.

00:56:46.850 --> 00:56:50.570 align:middle line:90%


00:56:50.570 --> 00:56:53.990 align:middle line:90%
So what's my simplified problem?

00:56:53.990 --> 00:56:59.090 align:middle line:84%
I'm maximizing c equals q plus
r minus k of q hat minus k

00:56:59.090 --> 00:57:02.510 align:middle line:84%
of r hat, and my
two constraints are

00:57:02.510 --> 00:57:06.320 align:middle line:84%
that r hat has to be
less than or equal to r,

00:57:06.320 --> 00:57:11.130 align:middle line:84%
and this bottom one, the IC
one, holds with equality.

00:57:11.130 --> 00:57:19.228 align:middle line:90%


00:57:19.228 --> 00:57:22.300 align:middle line:84%
So again, to simplify
this problem,

00:57:22.300 --> 00:57:27.670 align:middle line:84%
I now take this expression
for what k of q hat is,

00:57:27.670 --> 00:57:29.780 align:middle line:90%
and I plug this in here.

00:57:29.780 --> 00:57:33.580 align:middle line:84%
So this is k of q hat equals
this plus this minus this.

00:57:33.580 --> 00:57:35.980 align:middle line:84%
And so when I do
that, the equation

00:57:35.980 --> 00:57:46.780 align:middle line:84%
becomes q plus r minus this, so
minus k of q minus beta k of r,

00:57:46.780 --> 00:57:50.180 align:middle line:84%
and then I get a minus
1 minus beta k of r hat.

00:57:50.180 --> 00:57:52.600 align:middle line:84%
So I maximizing that
subject to the constraint

00:57:52.600 --> 00:57:55.155 align:middle line:84%
that r hat is less
than or equal to r.

00:57:55.155 --> 00:57:57.840 align:middle line:90%


00:57:57.840 --> 00:58:00.000 align:middle line:84%
I, again, look at
this expression

00:58:00.000 --> 00:58:01.950 align:middle line:84%
and I recognize
that the r hat only

00:58:01.950 --> 00:58:04.470 align:middle line:84%
appears one way, one
place, and the r hat

00:58:04.470 --> 00:58:07.200 align:middle line:84%
has got a positive
constant times

00:58:07.200 --> 00:58:09.040 align:middle line:90%
an increasing function of r hat.

00:58:09.040 --> 00:58:11.970 align:middle line:84%
So if my only constraint
is that r hat equals r,

00:58:11.970 --> 00:58:13.865 align:middle line:90%
I want to set r equals zero.

00:58:13.865 --> 00:58:16.580 align:middle line:90%


00:58:16.580 --> 00:58:18.400 align:middle line:84%
So that's where the
zero comes from.

00:58:18.400 --> 00:58:21.080 align:middle line:90%


00:58:21.080 --> 00:58:25.100 align:middle line:84%
And then I'm left with
a very simple problem,

00:58:25.100 --> 00:58:29.510 align:middle line:84%
which is just I'm trying
to maximize what's left.

00:58:29.510 --> 00:58:42.868 align:middle line:84%
I'm trying to maximize q plus r
minus k of q minus beta k of r.

00:58:42.868 --> 00:58:44.660 align:middle line:84%
And so then just the
first order conditions

00:58:44.660 --> 00:58:47.870 align:middle line:84%
say you set k prime
at q equal to 1

00:58:47.870 --> 00:58:51.490 align:middle line:84%
and you set k prime at
r equals to 1 over beta.

00:58:51.490 --> 00:58:55.915 align:middle line:90%


00:58:55.915 --> 00:58:58.530 align:middle line:84%
And so it's the k prime
at q equals 1 that

00:58:58.530 --> 00:59:01.680 align:middle line:90%
makes q the efficient level.

00:59:01.680 --> 00:59:03.900 align:middle line:90%
Good, so I did get that right.

00:59:03.900 --> 00:59:05.880 align:middle line:84%
If the efficient
level is 15,000,

00:59:05.880 --> 00:59:11.130 align:middle line:84%
you ask for the efficient
level at that period,

00:59:11.130 --> 00:59:13.660 align:middle line:84%
but then k prime and
r is 1 over beta,

00:59:13.660 --> 00:59:18.360 align:middle line:84%
so you ask for discretely
more money in this preference.

00:59:18.360 --> 00:59:22.185 align:middle line:90%
And so the model was that--

00:59:22.185 --> 00:59:25.410 align:middle line:84%
with the beta delta
hyperbolic consumers,

00:59:25.410 --> 00:59:26.980 align:middle line:90%
you didn't do any distorting.

00:59:26.980 --> 00:59:30.960 align:middle line:84%
But now with beta, beta
hat delta, with beta hat

00:59:30.960 --> 00:59:34.260 align:middle line:84%
arbitrarily close to beta,
you get this discrete jump

00:59:34.260 --> 00:59:37.470 align:middle line:84%
reflecting the 1
over beta discounting

00:59:37.470 --> 00:59:39.600 align:middle line:90%
versus the beta discounting.

00:59:39.600 --> 00:59:42.210 align:middle line:84%
And there's just no way that
you can come in as a lender

00:59:42.210 --> 00:59:43.710 align:middle line:84%
and compete in
this market and not

00:59:43.710 --> 00:59:47.360 align:middle line:84%
exploit the consumers
because this is

00:59:47.360 --> 00:59:48.610 align:middle line:90%
what the consumers are seeing.

00:59:48.610 --> 00:59:51.120 align:middle line:84%
The consumers are seeing
this borrow 35,000,

00:59:51.120 --> 00:59:53.710 align:middle line:90%
repay 25,000 contract.

00:59:53.710 --> 00:59:56.000 align:middle line:84%
They think that's what
they're going to do.

00:59:56.000 --> 00:59:58.613 align:middle line:84%
I can't come in and say, no,
look, take a reasonable size

00:59:58.613 --> 01:00:01.030 align:middle line:84%
loan, pay it out evenly, because
they're going to be like,

01:00:01.030 --> 01:00:03.980 align:middle line:84%
no, I would like to
get the nicer car

01:00:03.980 --> 01:00:07.910 align:middle line:84%
and it's a huge financial win
for me to take this teaser deal.

01:00:07.910 --> 01:00:10.090 align:middle line:84%
Therefore, you can't compete
with that teaser deal

01:00:10.090 --> 01:00:12.325 align:middle line:84%
with the regular deal,
because if you're not

01:00:12.325 --> 01:00:14.200 align:middle line:84%
exploiting the consumers,
you can't make them

01:00:14.200 --> 01:00:15.575 align:middle line:84%
that outlandish
promise of you're

01:00:15.575 --> 01:00:19.520 align:middle line:84%
going to get all this money
and repay this little amount.

01:00:19.520 --> 01:00:20.730 align:middle line:90%
Next paper I want to do.

01:00:20.730 --> 01:00:23.395 align:middle line:84%
I guess I won't get through
all of these today, but.

01:00:23.395 --> 01:00:26.340 align:middle line:90%


01:00:26.340 --> 01:00:28.410 align:middle line:84%
So this is a paper
by Michael Grubb,

01:00:28.410 --> 01:00:33.394 align:middle line:84%
"Selling to Overconfident
Consumers," which has a--

01:00:33.394 --> 01:00:36.480 align:middle line:84%
it's based on an
overconfidence bias.

01:00:36.480 --> 01:00:39.030 align:middle line:84%
People think they know more
about their future self

01:00:39.030 --> 01:00:40.410 align:middle line:90%
than they do.

01:00:40.410 --> 01:00:44.483 align:middle line:84%
And what he's
trying to explain is

01:00:44.483 --> 01:00:46.650 align:middle line:84%
one of the things I did
early on in monopoly pricing

01:00:46.650 --> 01:00:47.820 align:middle line:90%
is two part tariffs.

01:00:47.820 --> 01:00:51.090 align:middle line:84%
Like at Disneyland, you pay
$100 to get into the park

01:00:51.090 --> 01:00:53.730 align:middle line:84%
and then every ride
is free, or you

01:00:53.730 --> 01:00:57.120 align:middle line:84%
have something where you
pay some upfront cost

01:00:57.120 --> 01:00:59.340 align:middle line:84%
and then you pay a
low cost per unit

01:00:59.340 --> 01:01:00.900 align:middle line:84%
and they extract
all your surplus

01:01:00.900 --> 01:01:04.475 align:middle line:84%
you're getting from price equals
cost in a fixed fee up front.

01:01:04.475 --> 01:01:07.662 align:middle line:90%


01:01:07.662 --> 01:01:10.730 align:middle line:84%
What Grubb noticed is
that there are actually

01:01:10.730 --> 01:01:12.770 align:middle line:84%
a lot of applications
where what we seem to see

01:01:12.770 --> 01:01:17.570 align:middle line:84%
is not two part tariffs,
but three part tariffs.

01:01:17.570 --> 01:01:20.600 align:middle line:84%
So the three part tariff
is this payment scheme

01:01:20.600 --> 01:01:24.320 align:middle line:84%
where you have an upfront
payment for the good,

01:01:24.320 --> 01:01:26.960 align:middle line:84%
and then some number
of units of the good

01:01:26.960 --> 01:01:31.460 align:middle line:84%
sold at below cost, and
then additional units

01:01:31.460 --> 01:01:34.340 align:middle line:84%
beyond that sold at
an above cost price,

01:01:34.340 --> 01:01:36.310 align:middle line:84%
and often a very,
very high price.

01:01:36.310 --> 01:01:38.600 align:middle line:90%
So examples he gives.

01:01:38.600 --> 01:01:41.240 align:middle line:84%
Some of them, like the
credit card example,

01:01:41.240 --> 01:01:44.090 align:middle line:84%
you pay some upfront fee
to transfer your money

01:01:44.090 --> 01:01:46.870 align:middle line:90%
to the new credit card.

01:01:46.870 --> 01:01:49.565 align:middle line:84%
They'll give you six
months interest free,

01:01:49.565 --> 01:01:51.190 align:middle line:84%
and then after the
six months are over,

01:01:51.190 --> 01:01:54.650 align:middle line:84%
they're going to start
charging you 19.8%.

01:01:54.650 --> 01:01:59.030 align:middle line:84%
Or you lease a car, you pay
$12,000 for the three-year car

01:01:59.030 --> 01:02:00.792 align:middle line:90%
lease.

01:02:00.792 --> 01:02:02.750 align:middle line:84%
Car leases-- I don't know
if you've done these.

01:02:02.750 --> 01:02:04.880 align:middle line:84%
Car leases do have
mileage limits on them.

01:02:04.880 --> 01:02:06.950 align:middle line:84%
Like, because you lease
the car for three years,

01:02:06.950 --> 01:02:09.570 align:middle line:84%
you get 36,000
miles included free.

01:02:09.570 --> 01:02:11.990 align:middle line:84%
They don't charge you for
the wear and tear on the car.

01:02:11.990 --> 01:02:16.370 align:middle line:84%
But then if you drive the
car more than 36,000 miles,

01:02:16.370 --> 01:02:19.550 align:middle line:90%
you pay an extra $0.29 a mile.

01:02:19.550 --> 01:02:21.770 align:middle line:84%
Actually, maybe for you
guys, renting U-Haul trucks

01:02:21.770 --> 01:02:22.470 align:middle line:90%
works this way.

01:02:22.470 --> 01:02:24.762 align:middle line:84%
I don't know if you've ever
rented a U-Haul truck where

01:02:24.762 --> 01:02:29.900 align:middle line:84%
it's like you rent the truck
and it's 99.99 for the truck,

01:02:29.900 --> 01:02:33.150 align:middle line:84%
up to 150 miles of use
of the truck is free.

01:02:33.150 --> 01:02:36.770 align:middle line:84%
If you go over to 150
miles on the truck,

01:02:36.770 --> 01:02:40.520 align:middle line:84%
it's like $0.79 a mile or
something for every mile

01:02:40.520 --> 01:02:42.920 align:middle line:84%
that you charge, and you run
up this enormous over charge

01:02:42.920 --> 01:02:45.890 align:middle line:90%
on the U-Haul trucks.

01:02:45.890 --> 01:02:50.990 align:middle line:84%
Cell phones back in the
day, you paid 39.99 a month.

01:02:50.990 --> 01:02:53.940 align:middle line:84%
You would have two
gigabytes of data free.

01:02:53.940 --> 01:02:56.810 align:middle line:84%
Once you used up your
two gigabytes of usage,

01:02:56.810 --> 01:02:58.670 align:middle line:84%
they start charging
you some extra amount

01:02:58.670 --> 01:02:59.637 align:middle line:90%
of money per megabyte.

01:02:59.637 --> 01:03:01.220 align:middle line:84%
That doesn't sound
so big, but then it

01:03:01.220 --> 01:03:04.310 align:middle line:84%
becomes an enormous charge
for the overage on your data

01:03:04.310 --> 01:03:05.310 align:middle line:90%
overage.

01:03:05.310 --> 01:03:08.790 align:middle line:90%


01:03:08.790 --> 01:03:11.250 align:middle line:84%
Grubb suggests that
why do we see these--

01:03:11.250 --> 01:03:12.780 align:middle line:84%
we know why we
would see a two part

01:03:12.780 --> 01:03:15.220 align:middle line:84%
tariff, because it's efficient
to price things at cost.

01:03:15.220 --> 01:03:17.410 align:middle line:84%
Why would we see a
three part tariff?

01:03:17.410 --> 01:03:21.060 align:middle line:84%
And Grubb's argument is that
we see three part tariffs

01:03:21.060 --> 01:03:23.610 align:middle line:84%
because it's an
exploitation of consumers

01:03:23.610 --> 01:03:26.070 align:middle line:84%
who think they're better
than they are at predicting

01:03:26.070 --> 01:03:27.245 align:middle line:90%
their future consumption.

01:03:27.245 --> 01:03:28.620 align:middle line:84%
Just like you
think you're better

01:03:28.620 --> 01:03:31.470 align:middle line:84%
than you are at predicting
how far Buenos Aires is,

01:03:31.470 --> 01:03:33.210 align:middle line:84%
you think you're
better than you are

01:03:33.210 --> 01:03:35.760 align:middle line:84%
at predicting how
many gigabytes of data

01:03:35.760 --> 01:03:38.100 align:middle line:84%
or how many miles am I going
to drive the U-Haul truck

01:03:38.100 --> 01:03:41.500 align:middle line:90%
or something like that.

01:03:41.500 --> 01:03:46.330 align:middle line:84%
So he gives the simple example
about where the three part

01:03:46.330 --> 01:03:50.110 align:middle line:84%
tariffs would come from,
which is this straightforward

01:03:50.110 --> 01:03:51.350 align:middle line:90%
numerical thing.

01:03:51.350 --> 01:03:54.250 align:middle line:84%
So suppose firms
have fixed costs

01:03:54.250 --> 01:03:58.270 align:middle line:84%
of $50 of serving
the consumer and then

01:03:58.270 --> 01:04:02.110 align:middle line:84%
they have marginal
costs of $0.05 per unit

01:04:02.110 --> 01:04:03.110 align:middle line:90%
of serving the consumer.

01:04:03.110 --> 01:04:06.790 align:middle line:84%
So this could be
cost of providing you

01:04:06.790 --> 01:04:09.070 align:middle line:84%
with a cell phone and then
marginal cost per megabyte

01:04:09.070 --> 01:04:11.350 align:middle line:84%
or cost of renting
you the car and then

01:04:11.350 --> 01:04:14.900 align:middle line:84%
cost per mile of having
you drive the car.

01:04:14.900 --> 01:04:17.810 align:middle line:84%
And assume that the firm can
offer a non-linear tariff

01:04:17.810 --> 01:04:19.760 align:middle line:90%
t of x.

01:04:19.760 --> 01:04:23.900 align:middle line:84%
Assume that consumers get
benefits of $0.45 per unit

01:04:23.900 --> 01:04:26.540 align:middle line:84%
for theta units,
where theta is either

01:04:26.540 --> 01:04:30.700 align:middle line:84%
100, 400, or 700, and all
three are equally likely.

01:04:30.700 --> 01:04:33.205 align:middle line:90%


01:04:33.205 --> 01:04:35.510 align:middle line:84%
Either I get my
phone and then I'm

01:04:35.510 --> 01:04:38.828 align:middle line:84%
going to have some number of
megabytes of data I want to use,

01:04:38.828 --> 01:04:41.120 align:middle line:84%
and I don't know which of
those three it's going to be,

01:04:41.120 --> 01:04:42.662 align:middle line:84%
but whatever it is,
I'm going to have

01:04:42.662 --> 01:04:45.580 align:middle line:84%
just some inherent need for the
good that I want to use those.

01:04:45.580 --> 01:04:48.750 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to get $0.45 per
unit using theta units,

01:04:48.750 --> 01:04:51.200 align:middle line:84%
and then I'm not going to
want more than theta units.

01:04:51.200 --> 01:04:53.360 align:middle line:84%
Or the U-Haul rental
truck, I'm either

01:04:53.360 --> 01:04:57.060 align:middle line:84%
going to want to drive it 100
miles, 400 miles, or 700 miles.

01:04:57.060 --> 01:04:59.310 align:middle line:84%
I don't know which of those
three it's going to be.

01:04:59.310 --> 01:05:01.520 align:middle line:84%
However many I get,
I get this high value

01:05:01.520 --> 01:05:03.680 align:middle line:84%
for using it for those
miles and then no value

01:05:03.680 --> 01:05:05.360 align:middle line:90%
for using it beyond that.

01:05:05.360 --> 01:05:08.900 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, all
three are considered

01:05:08.900 --> 01:05:11.360 align:middle line:84%
equally likely by the
consumer when they purchase,

01:05:11.360 --> 01:05:13.878 align:middle line:84%
and then after they
purchase, they learn theta

01:05:13.878 --> 01:05:16.170 align:middle line:84%
and then they choose how much
to actually use the thing

01:05:16.170 --> 01:05:19.170 align:middle line:90%
that they've purchased.

01:05:19.170 --> 01:05:19.680 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:05:19.680 --> 01:05:22.590 align:middle line:84%
What would happen with
rational consumers?

01:05:22.590 --> 01:05:26.460 align:middle line:84%
With rational consumers,
we do a two part tariff.

01:05:26.460 --> 01:05:30.120 align:middle line:84%
The cost of providing the
units to the seller is $0.05.

01:05:30.120 --> 01:05:33.120 align:middle line:84%
So you charge
consumers $0.05 a unit,

01:05:33.120 --> 01:05:36.000 align:middle line:84%
and then you extract
all the ex-ante surplus

01:05:36.000 --> 01:05:37.390 align:middle line:90%
through a fixed fee.

01:05:37.390 --> 01:05:39.180 align:middle line:84%
And if consumers are
going to, on average,

01:05:39.180 --> 01:05:46.590 align:middle line:84%
use 400 units and they're
getting $0.40 per unit

01:05:46.590 --> 01:05:50.350 align:middle line:84%
of surplus, 400
times $0.40 is $160.

01:05:50.350 --> 01:05:56.290 align:middle line:84%
So the rational consumer plan,
you would do 160 plus $0.05.

01:05:56.290 --> 01:05:58.140 align:middle line:84%
With perfectly
competitive firms,

01:05:58.140 --> 01:06:00.240 align:middle line:84%
you'd, again, use the
$0.5 fee and you'd just

01:06:00.240 --> 01:06:03.390 align:middle line:84%
be charging the 50 up
front to charge cost.

01:06:03.390 --> 01:06:06.485 align:middle line:84%
And this is socially
efficient pricing.

01:06:06.485 --> 01:06:11.110 align:middle line:90%


01:06:11.110 --> 01:06:16.790 align:middle line:84%
What happens with the
monopolist Grubb's observation

01:06:16.790 --> 01:06:18.970 align:middle line:84%
on what happens with
the monopolist is

01:06:18.970 --> 01:06:22.420 align:middle line:84%
a monopolist in this problem
would use a three part tariff.

01:06:22.420 --> 01:06:23.950 align:middle line:90%
They would charge you--

01:06:23.950 --> 01:06:26.510 align:middle line:84%
instead of $160
plus $0.05 a minute,

01:06:26.510 --> 01:06:29.840 align:middle line:90%
they'd charge you $180 up front.

01:06:29.840 --> 01:06:35.150 align:middle line:84%
The first 400 units would be
free, and then units beyond 400,

01:06:35.150 --> 01:06:37.160 align:middle line:90%
they would charge you $0.45.

01:06:37.160 --> 01:06:41.430 align:middle line:84%
So we just get this very
high, steep three part tariff.

01:06:41.430 --> 01:06:45.110 align:middle line:84%
We start high fixed fee,
no cost for 400 units,

01:06:45.110 --> 01:06:50.190 align:middle line:84%
and then steeply increasing
price beyond 400.

01:06:50.190 --> 01:06:51.660 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:06:51.660 --> 01:06:55.230 align:middle line:90%
Why do we do this?

01:06:55.230 --> 01:06:59.280 align:middle line:84%
First, why do you charge
nothing for the first 400 units?

01:06:59.280 --> 01:07:00.570 align:middle line:90%
Oh, sorry.

01:07:00.570 --> 01:07:01.670 align:middle line:90%
I didn't say this.

01:07:01.670 --> 01:07:03.420 align:middle line:84%
He's going to analyze
this model where you

01:07:03.420 --> 01:07:05.160 align:middle line:90%
have overconfident consumers.

01:07:05.160 --> 01:07:06.660 align:middle line:84%
The starkest model
of overconfidence

01:07:06.660 --> 01:07:08.940 align:middle line:84%
is the consumers think
they're going to need

01:07:08.940 --> 01:07:11.320 align:middle line:90%
400 units with probability one.

01:07:11.320 --> 01:07:13.830 align:middle line:84%
So they don't realize that
they might only need 100

01:07:13.830 --> 01:07:15.600 align:middle line:90%
or they might need 700.

01:07:15.600 --> 01:07:18.570 align:middle line:84%
So they think they're going to
use 400 with probability one.

01:07:18.570 --> 01:07:19.080 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:07:19.080 --> 01:07:22.480 align:middle line:84%
Why do I charge zero
for the first 400 units?

01:07:22.480 --> 01:07:27.030 align:middle line:84%
Why do I do 180 and then
zero for the first 400 units?

01:07:27.030 --> 01:07:35.040 align:middle line:84%
Why don't I do 160 up front and
$0.05 for the first 400 units

01:07:35.040 --> 01:07:38.120 align:middle line:84%
so you pay 180 if you use
400 but less if you don't?

01:07:38.120 --> 01:07:44.060 align:middle line:84%
Well, the point is if I'm going
to charge you a positive amount,

01:07:44.060 --> 01:07:47.660 align:middle line:84%
like $0.05 for the
first 400 units,

01:07:47.660 --> 01:07:51.290 align:middle line:84%
I'm only going to charge you
160 up front instead of 180

01:07:51.290 --> 01:07:52.580 align:middle line:90%
up front.

01:07:52.580 --> 01:07:55.790 align:middle line:84%
And then when you
actually use 100 units,

01:07:55.790 --> 01:07:58.940 align:middle line:84%
you're going to
end up paying less.

01:07:58.940 --> 01:08:05.420 align:middle line:84%
So you might use 100, you might
use 400, you might use 700.

01:08:05.420 --> 01:08:10.730 align:middle line:84%
So if I used a plan where
I charged you a per unit

01:08:10.730 --> 01:08:15.260 align:middle line:84%
fee for the first 400
units, I have to give you

01:08:15.260 --> 01:08:18.350 align:middle line:90%
the full discount of--

01:08:18.350 --> 01:08:23.540 align:middle line:84%
I have to give you 400 delta
p back in the fixed fee.

01:08:23.540 --> 01:08:26.770 align:middle line:84%
And then I give you 400 delta
p back in the fixed fee,

01:08:26.770 --> 01:08:30.560 align:middle line:84%
but then I only get
300 p in extra revenue

01:08:30.560 --> 01:08:34.100 align:middle line:84%
because sometimes
you get a better

01:08:34.100 --> 01:08:35.660 align:middle line:90%
deal than you're expecting.

01:08:35.660 --> 01:08:37.910 align:middle line:84%
Since you don't expect
the deal, you're

01:08:37.910 --> 01:08:41.569 align:middle line:84%
not going to pay me up front
for that, that option of there's

01:08:41.569 --> 01:08:44.510 align:middle line:84%
some chance I get
to save and only

01:08:44.510 --> 01:08:47.430 align:middle line:90%
pay you for 100 instead of 400.

01:08:47.430 --> 01:08:51.290 align:middle line:84%
And so because the consumers
aren't willing to pay you,

01:08:51.290 --> 01:08:53.359 align:middle line:84%
it's always better
to charge them

01:08:53.359 --> 01:08:55.972 align:middle line:84%
the full price even for the
units they're not going--

01:08:55.972 --> 01:08:57.680 align:middle line:84%
this is like exploiting
the consumers who

01:08:57.680 --> 01:08:59.930 align:middle line:84%
think they're going to go
to the gym every single day.

01:08:59.930 --> 01:09:03.698 align:middle line:84%
You always want to charge them
that amount up front, and then

01:09:03.698 --> 01:09:06.240 align:middle line:84%
say, OK, I'm giving you this
great deal where you get the 400

01:09:06.240 --> 01:09:07.946 align:middle line:84%
units for free, and
then you're going

01:09:07.946 --> 01:09:09.779 align:middle line:84%
to pay me in advance
for all those 400 units

01:09:09.779 --> 01:09:10.987 align:middle line:90%
that you're getting for free.

01:09:10.987 --> 01:09:13.520 align:middle line:90%


01:09:13.520 --> 01:09:19.490 align:middle line:84%
And then why do firms charge
for $0.45 for units beyond 400?

01:09:19.490 --> 01:09:21.490 align:middle line:84%
Well, the consumers are
sure they're never going

01:09:21.490 --> 01:09:24.007 align:middle line:90%
to use any units beyond 400.

01:09:24.007 --> 01:09:26.590 align:middle line:84%
So if you offer them a discount,
you can have units beyond 400

01:09:26.590 --> 01:09:29.620 align:middle line:84%
for $0.40, they're going to pay
you nothing up front for that

01:09:29.620 --> 01:09:31.930 align:middle line:84%
discount because they think
they're not going to use

01:09:31.930 --> 01:09:33.260 align:middle line:90%
the extra units.

01:09:33.260 --> 01:09:35.843 align:middle line:84%
And so if they think they're not
going to use the extra units,

01:09:35.843 --> 01:09:38.609 align:middle line:84%
they won't pay anything for the
lower price and the extra units,

01:09:38.609 --> 01:09:40.720 align:middle line:84%
so you might as well
keep raising the price

01:09:40.720 --> 01:09:44.240 align:middle line:84%
on the extra units
until they get to $0.45.

01:09:44.240 --> 01:09:46.383 align:middle line:84%
You don't go beyond 45
because then ex-post,

01:09:46.383 --> 01:09:47.800 align:middle line:84%
they're not even
going to buy them

01:09:47.800 --> 01:09:49.840 align:middle line:84%
and they're just going to
not drive the rental truck

01:09:49.840 --> 01:09:51.135 align:middle line:90%
for the extra miles they need.

01:09:51.135 --> 01:09:54.229 align:middle line:90%


01:09:54.229 --> 01:09:57.870 align:middle line:84%
So what we get is this
three part tariff.

01:09:57.870 --> 01:10:00.530 align:middle line:84%
And if you had perfectly
competitive firms,

01:10:00.530 --> 01:10:02.480 align:middle line:84%
perfectly competitive
firms would-- again,

01:10:02.480 --> 01:10:04.730 align:middle line:84%
they're forced to
exploit the consumers,

01:10:04.730 --> 01:10:07.040 align:middle line:84%
and so they would do
something like that.

01:10:07.040 --> 01:10:09.140 align:middle line:84%
So you would give
all the surplus back

01:10:09.140 --> 01:10:11.700 align:middle line:84%
to the consumers with
a really low fixed fee.

01:10:11.700 --> 01:10:14.180 align:middle line:84%
But still, what makes it
most attractive to consumers

01:10:14.180 --> 01:10:17.960 align:middle line:84%
is to design the package
so that they think

01:10:17.960 --> 01:10:19.550 align:middle line:90%
they're going to pay the least.

01:10:19.550 --> 01:10:22.130 align:middle line:84%
Which is give them the
big discount up front,

01:10:22.130 --> 01:10:24.500 align:middle line:84%
tell them the 400 are
included, and tell them

01:10:24.500 --> 01:10:28.200 align:middle line:84%
we are going to charge you
these high over charges.

01:10:28.200 --> 01:10:29.950 align:middle line:84%
But you think you're
not going to pay them

01:10:29.950 --> 01:10:31.470 align:middle line:90%
so they don't care about that.

01:10:31.470 --> 01:10:34.760 align:middle line:84%
And so this is what lets the
competitive sellers offer

01:10:34.760 --> 01:10:35.950 align:middle line:90%
the best possible package.

01:10:35.950 --> 01:10:42.110 align:middle line:90%


01:10:42.110 --> 01:10:42.770 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:10:42.770 --> 01:10:44.870 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: How would you
tell this sort of behavior

01:10:44.870 --> 01:10:48.230 align:middle line:84%
out from something like a
increasing [? call ?] structure?

01:10:48.230 --> 01:10:49.700 align:middle line:90%
GLENN ELLISON: Yeah.

01:10:49.700 --> 01:10:54.680 align:middle line:84%
You know, obviously, I think in
some of these examples, like--

01:10:54.680 --> 01:10:57.600 align:middle line:84%
some examples that he gives
are better than others on that.

01:10:57.600 --> 01:11:03.050 align:middle line:84%
So for instance, the
cell phone minute plan.

01:11:03.050 --> 01:11:07.620 align:middle line:84%
The megabytes are system
wide, not customer wide.

01:11:07.620 --> 01:11:12.917 align:middle line:84%
So it can't be that you
using more megabytes beyond--

01:11:12.917 --> 01:11:14.750 align:middle line:84%
there, you know that
their cost per megabyte

01:11:14.750 --> 01:11:17.210 align:middle line:84%
has got to be
linear because it's

01:11:17.210 --> 01:11:18.600 align:middle line:90%
averaged across all consumers.

01:11:18.600 --> 01:11:20.660 align:middle line:84%
It's a network
capacity constraint.

01:11:20.660 --> 01:11:24.170 align:middle line:84%
For ones like the car lease,
you could say, is there

01:11:24.170 --> 01:11:26.660 align:middle line:84%
something about
the wear and tear

01:11:26.660 --> 01:11:31.490 align:middle line:84%
on the car that increases as
the car is driven more miles in?

01:11:31.490 --> 01:11:34.460 align:middle line:84%
Is there some
increasing wear and tear

01:11:34.460 --> 01:11:37.490 align:middle line:84%
on the car or the rental truck
because it goes more miles?

01:11:37.490 --> 01:11:40.890 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, that one you would have
to have something to tell apart.

01:11:40.890 --> 01:11:43.520 align:middle line:84%
Again, I think the
credit card one,

01:11:43.520 --> 01:11:47.150 align:middle line:84%
it's going to be harder for
a discontinuity in months

01:11:47.150 --> 01:11:48.890 align:middle line:90%
or whatever.

01:11:48.890 --> 01:11:51.680 align:middle line:84%
What Grubb does
have in his paper

01:11:51.680 --> 01:11:56.480 align:middle line:90%
is he has an empirical section.

01:11:56.480 --> 01:12:00.320 align:middle line:84%
We say, yeah, what did
Grubb do to get this paper?

01:12:00.320 --> 01:12:03.470 align:middle line:84%
Obviously the story I
told you is fairly simple.

01:12:03.470 --> 01:12:05.640 align:middle line:84%
How do you turn this
into an AER paper?

01:12:05.640 --> 01:12:08.090 align:middle line:84%
This is like your idea
you come to me with

01:12:08.090 --> 01:12:10.220 align:middle line:84%
and you explain it
and it's like, OK,

01:12:10.220 --> 01:12:11.990 align:middle line:90%
that's a nice, cute example.

01:12:11.990 --> 01:12:14.600 align:middle line:90%
That's not a paper in the AER.

01:12:14.600 --> 01:12:18.770 align:middle line:84%
He has a much more general
model where he has--

01:12:18.770 --> 01:12:20.030 align:middle line:90%
you have a theta.

01:12:20.030 --> 01:12:24.440 align:middle line:90%
The theta has some distribution.

01:12:24.440 --> 01:12:29.570 align:middle line:84%
Your consumers have some
belief about theta f star.

01:12:29.570 --> 01:12:34.250 align:middle line:84%
When f star differs
from f, it's kind

01:12:34.250 --> 01:12:38.510 align:middle line:84%
of mean preserving spread or
single crossing condition.

01:12:38.510 --> 01:12:39.600 align:middle line:90%
What happens?

01:12:39.600 --> 01:12:42.960 align:middle line:84%
He goes out and solves the model
and says, OK, this simple model

01:12:42.960 --> 01:12:44.960 align:middle line:84%
I had had this
three part tariff,

01:12:44.960 --> 01:12:47.300 align:middle line:84%
but I do this general
model and what I show

01:12:47.300 --> 01:12:51.002 align:middle line:84%
is that the optimal solution
is not like a three part-- not

01:12:51.002 --> 01:12:52.460 align:middle line:84%
exactly a three
part tariff, but it

01:12:52.460 --> 01:12:56.330 align:middle line:84%
is something that looks kind
of like a three part tariff.

01:12:56.330 --> 01:12:57.860 align:middle line:84%
But then the second
thing he does

01:12:57.860 --> 01:13:00.513 align:middle line:84%
is he has this data set
on cell phone users.

01:13:00.513 --> 01:13:05.000 align:middle line:90%


01:13:05.000 --> 01:13:07.730 align:middle line:84%
I don't think he says in the
paper, but I think this was--

01:13:07.730 --> 01:13:10.340 align:middle line:84%
there was a time when
cell phones were new,

01:13:10.340 --> 01:13:15.140 align:middle line:84%
when universities provided cell
phone plans to their students

01:13:15.140 --> 01:13:17.390 align:middle line:84%
and you could get your cell
phone not through Verizon,

01:13:17.390 --> 01:13:18.680 align:middle line:90%
but through MIT.

01:13:18.680 --> 01:13:21.020 align:middle line:84%
You'd get an MIT
cell phone and MIT

01:13:21.020 --> 01:13:22.820 align:middle line:84%
would negotiate a
deal with Verizon

01:13:22.820 --> 01:13:26.510 align:middle line:84%
to offer discounted cell phone
service for its students.

01:13:26.510 --> 01:13:28.588 align:middle line:84%
I take it that these
were probably Stanford--

01:13:28.588 --> 01:13:29.630 align:middle line:90%
Doesn't say in the paper.

01:13:29.630 --> 01:13:31.963 align:middle line:84%
I take it these are probably
Stanford student cell phone

01:13:31.963 --> 01:13:32.600 align:middle line:90%
plans.

01:13:32.600 --> 01:13:35.540 align:middle line:84%
But they had four
cell phone plans,

01:13:35.540 --> 01:13:41.000 align:middle line:84%
one where you get this amount,
just a flat fee per minute.

01:13:41.000 --> 01:13:45.060 align:middle line:84%
Small upfront charge, and then
a flat fee per minute used.

01:13:45.060 --> 01:13:49.160 align:middle line:84%
And then you had plans that had
different numbers of included

01:13:49.160 --> 01:13:51.950 align:middle line:84%
usage times like,
at the time it might

01:13:51.950 --> 01:13:55.320 align:middle line:84%
have been 100 minutes of
usage or 400 minutes of usage

01:13:55.320 --> 01:13:58.050 align:middle line:90%
or 700 minutes of usage.

01:13:58.050 --> 01:14:01.000 align:middle line:84%
And so each of these plans
was a three part tariff.

01:14:01.000 --> 01:14:02.380 align:middle line:90%
We paid some higher upfront fee.

01:14:02.380 --> 01:14:03.660 align:middle line:90%
You got the 100 minutes.

01:14:03.660 --> 01:14:05.040 align:middle line:84%
And then beyond
that, you started

01:14:05.040 --> 01:14:06.960 align:middle line:90%
to pay these overage charges.

01:14:06.960 --> 01:14:11.190 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, this unnamed
university offering four plans

01:14:11.190 --> 01:14:12.500 align:middle line:90%
had a structure like that.

01:14:12.500 --> 01:14:15.240 align:middle line:90%


01:14:15.240 --> 01:14:19.770 align:middle line:84%
He got data on what plans
people signed up for

01:14:19.770 --> 01:14:24.480 align:middle line:84%
and how much usage
they actually made.

01:14:24.480 --> 01:14:25.840 align:middle line:90%
So what are some of the results?

01:14:25.840 --> 01:14:28.740 align:middle line:84%
So plan one customers--
let's suppose

01:14:28.740 --> 01:14:33.660 align:middle line:84%
that this is plan one, one
of the most popular plans.

01:14:33.660 --> 01:14:37.560 align:middle line:84%
What he finds is that
customers on that plan,

01:14:37.560 --> 01:14:42.120 align:middle line:84%
56% of the time they
underutilized their plan

01:14:42.120 --> 01:14:46.320 align:middle line:84%
and used so few minutes that
they would have been better

01:14:46.320 --> 01:14:49.110 align:middle line:90%
off on the per minute plan.

01:14:49.110 --> 01:14:52.620 align:middle line:84%
16% of the time
they ran up overages

01:14:52.620 --> 01:14:57.390 align:middle line:84%
and were on this part of the
tariff, and only 28% of the time

01:14:57.390 --> 01:15:00.240 align:middle line:84%
were in of sweet spot where
plan one is actually better

01:15:00.240 --> 01:15:03.570 align:middle line:90%
than a regular monthly plan.

01:15:03.570 --> 01:15:08.370 align:middle line:84%
And so when he estimates
what the consumers would

01:15:08.370 --> 01:15:11.460 align:middle line:84%
have paid had they just
all bought the linear plan,

01:15:11.460 --> 01:15:15.768 align:middle line:84%
he finds that, on average,
the saving was 42%.

01:15:15.768 --> 01:15:18.060 align:middle line:84%
Now that doesn't mean that
any individual consumer made

01:15:18.060 --> 01:15:19.500 align:middle line:84%
a mistake, because each
individual consumer,

01:15:19.500 --> 01:15:21.042 align:middle line:84%
you don't know what
their belief was.

01:15:21.042 --> 01:15:23.550 align:middle line:84%
But certainly if, on
average, all the consumers

01:15:23.550 --> 01:15:27.510 align:middle line:84%
would have saved 40% by just
buying the flat rate plan,

01:15:27.510 --> 01:15:32.430 align:middle line:84%
then some consumers must be
irrational buying these plans

01:15:32.430 --> 01:15:34.500 align:middle line:84%
and must be getting
exploited by them.

01:15:34.500 --> 01:15:37.330 align:middle line:84%
And then similar calculation
for the plan two customers.

01:15:37.330 --> 01:15:43.530 align:middle line:84%
Again, there even more of
them didn't hit this point.

01:15:43.530 --> 01:15:45.330 align:middle line:84%
Again, there's a
small percentage who

01:15:45.330 --> 01:15:47.190 align:middle line:84%
hit the overage
range, but the overage

01:15:47.190 --> 01:15:50.940 align:middle line:84%
range, they pay so much
more that they account for--

01:15:50.940 --> 01:15:53.427 align:middle line:84%
they're paying two and
a half times as much

01:15:53.427 --> 01:15:55.510 align:middle line:84%
as they would pay had they
gotten the linear plan.

01:15:55.510 --> 01:15:59.940 align:middle line:84%
So their overage
customers are way up here.

01:15:59.940 --> 01:16:04.985 align:middle line:84%
And the overages account
for 23% of salary revenues.

01:16:04.985 --> 01:16:10.930 align:middle line:90%


01:16:10.930 --> 01:16:15.610 align:middle line:84%
He's got something
in the paper arguing,

01:16:15.610 --> 01:16:18.320 align:middle line:84%
could this be due to
price discrimination

01:16:18.320 --> 01:16:19.820 align:middle line:84%
rather than to
exploiting consumers?

01:16:19.820 --> 01:16:22.403 align:middle line:84%
And then he has some conditions
on the distribution they would

01:16:22.403 --> 01:16:24.071 align:middle line:84%
need for this to be
price discrimination

01:16:24.071 --> 01:16:25.852 align:middle line:84%
and not exploiting
irrational consumers.

01:16:25.852 --> 01:16:28.310 align:middle line:84%
And he argues, no, this is
exploiting irrational consumers.

01:16:28.310 --> 01:16:31.210 align:middle line:90%


01:16:31.210 --> 01:16:31.783 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:16:31.783 --> 01:16:33.200 align:middle line:84%
I guess I'm out
of time for today.

01:16:33.200 --> 01:16:40.690 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, I have slides on
here on a couple other papers.

01:16:40.690 --> 01:16:43.240 align:middle line:84%
One I think is very
cute is this is just

01:16:43.240 --> 01:16:47.958 align:middle line:84%
another version of exploiting
the hyperbolic consumers,

01:16:47.958 --> 01:16:49.750 align:middle line:84%
noting that another
real constraint you get

01:16:49.750 --> 01:16:52.420 align:middle line:84%
with hyperbolic consumers
is that you can exploit

01:16:52.420 --> 01:16:55.420 align:middle line:84%
them even more if
they think they're

01:16:55.420 --> 01:16:57.260 align:middle line:90%
going to switch tomorrow.

01:16:57.260 --> 01:16:59.470 align:middle line:84%
And so a constraint that
hyperbolic consumers have

01:16:59.470 --> 01:17:03.160 align:middle line:84%
is not just that I think I'm
going to go to the gym more

01:17:03.160 --> 01:17:05.567 align:middle line:84%
than I am, but I think
I'm going to cancel.

01:17:05.567 --> 01:17:07.900 align:middle line:84%
As long as I have the option
to cancel my gym membership

01:17:07.900 --> 01:17:10.330 align:middle line:84%
tomorrow if I determine
I don't like the gym,

01:17:10.330 --> 01:17:11.840 align:middle line:84%
I can always just
cancel it tomorrow

01:17:11.840 --> 01:17:13.840 align:middle line:84%
so I might as well
stay signed up today.

01:17:13.840 --> 01:17:17.020 align:middle line:84%
And noting that this sort of
friendly option of letting

01:17:17.020 --> 01:17:20.357 align:middle line:84%
consumers cancel at any time
can be a very effective way

01:17:20.357 --> 01:17:21.940 align:middle line:84%
to get them to pay
much more than they

01:17:21.940 --> 01:17:25.330 align:middle line:84%
would pay if you didn't have
that extra option in there.

01:17:25.330 --> 01:17:26.290 align:middle line:90%
OK?

01:17:26.290 --> 01:17:28.960 align:middle line:84%
And then, yeah, I
think that's it.

01:17:28.960 --> 01:17:31.870 align:middle line:84%
That's all I'll talk
about for today.

01:17:31.870 --> 01:17:49.000 align:middle line:90%